window.LMS_LOAD && window.LMS_LOAD("fastapi-for-ai", {"topics":[{"key":"fastapi-for-ai/asgi-lifecycle","sources":["ASGI specification (asgiref project)","Official docs: Starlette","Official docs: Uvicorn"],"why":"WSGI, the previous Python web contract, is a synchronous function call: `app(environ, start_response)`\nreturns an iterable of bytes, and the server dedicates a thread or process to the request until that\niterable is exhausted. That model worked for millisecond database-backed pages. It breaks for AI\nservices: an LLM call can hold a connection for 30–120 seconds while the handler does nothing but wait\non a socket, token streaming wants to write partial output for the whole of that time, voice and agent\nUIs want WebSockets, and a single request often fans out to several provider calls at once. Under\nthread-per-request, 500 concurrent streaming chats means 500 mostly idle threads, each with a stack,\nall contending on the GIL for the little Python work there is, and no way to say \"wait for this socket\nwithout holding a thread\".\n\nThe naive fixes fail. \"Add more threads\" hits memory and scheduler cost long before 10k connections,\nand still offers no WebSocket or server-push semantics. \"Monkey-patch with gevent\" makes blocking\nlibraries cooperative by stealth, which is fragile with C extensions and GPU libraries. Neither gives a\nstartup/shutdown protocol, so model loading ends up at import time (slow tests, no failure signal) or\non the first request (one unlucky user pays the cold start).\n\nASGI replaces call-and-return with asynchronous message passing over an awaitable callable. One event\nloop can hold thousands of connections, each parked cheaply on `await`, and the protocol has explicit\nmessages for request body chunks, response chunks, disconnects, WebSocket frames and process lifecycle.\nThe cost is that every layer must speak the protocol, which is exactly what Starlette and FastAPI do\nfor you.","how":"### One callable, three kinds of scope\nAn ASGI application is `async def app(scope, receive, send)`. `scope` is a plain dict describing the\nconnection; its `type` is `\"http\"`, `\"websocket\"` or `\"lifespan\"`. For HTTP it also carries `method`,\n`path`, `query_string`, `headers` (a list of `(bytes, bytes)` pairs), `client`, `server` and a `state`\ndict. `receive` is an async callable that returns the next event from the server; `send` pushes an\nevent back. The server calls `app` once per connection: for HTTP/1.1 keep-alive, every request on the\nsocket is its own call with a fresh scope, so nothing leaks between requests unless you create it.\n\n### The lifespan connection\nWhen a worker process starts, the server opens one connection with `scope[\"type\"] == \"lifespan\"` and\ncalls the app. The app awaits `receive()` and gets `{\"type\": \"lifespan.startup\"}`; it runs startup\nwork, then sends `lifespan.startup.complete` (or `lifespan.startup.failed` with a message). It then\nparks on `receive()` until the server sends `lifespan.shutdown`, runs teardown, and answers\n`lifespan.shutdown.complete`. FastAPI's `lifespan` context manager is literally the code before and\nafter the `yield` in this exchange. Uvicorn completes the startup handshake *before* it starts\naccepting connections on its socket, so a worker whose startup fails never receives traffic, and a\nworker that is still loading a model is simply not serving yet. Uvicorn's `--lifespan` flag chooses\n`auto` (tolerate apps that do not implement the protocol), `on` (require it) or `off`.\n\n### An HTTP request, message by message\n1. The server parses the request line and headers, builds the scope and calls `app`.\n2. The app reads the body by looping on `receive()`: each `http.request` event carries a `body` chunk\n   and a `more_body` flag. FastAPI's JSON parsing and `UploadFile` streaming are both built on this\n   loop, which is why a body can be read only once.\n3. The app sends exactly one `http.response.start` with `status` and `headers`, then one or more\n   `http.response.body` events. Setting `more_body: True` keeps the response open, which is how\n   `StreamingResponse` emits tokens as they arrive; the final event has `more_body: False`.\n4. If the client goes away, the next `receive()` returns `http.disconnect`. Nothing is raised\n   automatically, *because* the app is the one doing the awaiting; this is why Starlette offers\n   `request.is_disconnected()` and why a token generator only notices a vanished client when it next\n   tries to send.\n\n### WebSockets\nA `websocket` scope begins with a `websocket.connect` event; the app answers `websocket.accept`\n(optionally with a subprotocol) or `websocket.close`. Frames then flow as `websocket.receive` /\n`websocket.send` events with `text` or `bytes`, until `websocket.disconnect`. The same callable\nsignature handles it, which is why a FastAPI app can serve REST and WebSocket routes from one process.\n\n### Where FastAPI sits in the chain\nFastAPI subclasses Starlette, and Starlette's `__call__` builds a stack on first use:\n`ServerErrorMiddleware` → your middleware → `ExceptionMiddleware` → `Router` → route → endpoint. Every\nlayer has the same `(scope, receive, send)` signature, so a middleware can wrap `send` to add a header\nor count bytes, or wrap `receive` to cap body size. The router sends lifespan scopes to the lifespan\nhandler and dispatches http/websocket scopes by path.\n\n### Processes and event loops\nOne worker process runs one event loop and one app instance. `uvicorn --workers 4` (or Gunicorn with a\nUvicorn worker class) forks four processes, each of which runs its own lifespan handshake and holds its\nown copies of anything loaded there. \"Load once\" therefore means once per worker, which matters when\nthe thing loaded is a multi-gigabyte model.","example":"A raw ASGI app with no framework, showing the lifespan handshake, body reading and a streamed response.\nRun it with `uvicorn asgi_demo:app --lifespan on` and `curl -N -d \"the kv cache stores keys\"\nlocalhost:8000` to watch words arrive one at a time. FastAPI's `lifespan=` and `StreamingResponse`\ngenerate exactly these messages for you.\n\n```python\nimport asyncio\n\nSTATE = {}\n\nasync def app(scope, receive, send):\n    if scope[\"type\"] == \"lifespan\":\n        while True:\n            msg = await receive()\n            if msg[\"type\"] == \"lifespan.startup\":\n                STATE[\"gen\"] = lambda text: (w + \" \" for w in text.split())  # stand-in for a model\n                await send({\"type\": \"lifespan.startup.complete\"})\n            elif msg[\"type\"] == \"lifespan.shutdown\":\n                STATE.clear()\n                await send({\"type\": \"lifespan.shutdown.complete\"})\n                return\n    elif scope[\"type\"] == \"http\":\n        body = b\"\"\n        while True:                                   # read the whole request body\n            msg = await receive()\n            body += msg.get(\"body\", b\"\")\n            if not msg.get(\"more_body\"):\n                break\n        await send({\"type\": \"http.response.start\", \"status\": 200,\n                    \"headers\": [(b\"content-type\", b\"text/plain\")]})\n        for token in STATE[\"gen\"](body.decode() or \"hello world\"):\n            await send({\"type\": \"http.response.body\", \"body\": token.encode(), \"more_body\": True})\n            await asyncio.sleep(0.2)                  # pretend each token costs time\n        await send({\"type\": \"http.response.body\", \"body\": b\"\", \"more_body\": False})\n```","tradeoffs":["One event loop serves thousands of idle connections cheaply, but a single blocking call anywhere stalls all of them; the win flips into a loss the moment CPU-bound inference runs on the loop.","The message protocol gives you streaming, WebSockets and disconnect events, at the price of every middleware having to handle `send`/`receive` correctly; a sloppy middleware can buffer a token stream into one lump.","Per-worker lifespan isolates failures and avoids cross-process locks, but multiplies memory for app-scoped models by the worker count; past one or two workers a separate model server usually wins.","A failing startup keeps a broken worker off the network, which is safe, but it also means a slow model load delays readiness; orchestrators need startup probes tuned to the load time."],"pitfalls":["Reading the request body twice (for example in a logging middleware and then in the endpoint); the second reader hangs or gets an empty body because `http.request` events are consumed once. Symptom: 422s or timeouts only when that middleware is enabled.","Assuming a client disconnect raises an exception in the handler; it does not, so a long LLM call keeps burning provider tokens for a user who left. Symptom: provider spend higher than responses actually delivered.","Treating `app.state` as shared memory across workers; each worker has its own, so caches and counters diverge. Symptom: rate limits or cache hit rates that look random under `--workers 4`.","Putting model loading at import time instead of the lifespan handshake; tests, migrations and CLI tools that import the module pay the load, and a load failure surfaces as an import error with no `startup.failed` message. Symptom: twenty-second test collection."],"when":"You do not choose ASGI versus WSGI per endpoint; you choose it per service, and for anything that\nstreams, holds long connections or awaits remote models the answer is ASGI. Reach for raw protocol\nknowledge when something behaves oddly at the edges: a streaming response that arrives all at once, a\ndisconnect that nobody noticed, a startup that hangs under Gunicorn, a body that reads as empty.\nMeasure event-loop lag (how late a no-op task scheduled every second actually runs) under load; it\ntells you whether something is blocking. In interviews, being able to walk through the lifespan and\nHTTP message sequence, and to say where FastAPI's lifespan, middleware and `StreamingResponse` plug\ninto it, separates people who have read the docs from people who have debugged a server.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes pure-ASGI middleware when `BaseHTTPMiddleware` breaks streaming, reads `http.disconnect` to cancel abandoned LLM calls, and keeps startup work inside lifespan.","architect":"Decides worker count versus a dedicated model server from the fact that lifespan state is per process, and sets probe timings around measured startup cost.","fde":"Diagnoses \"the stream only shows up at the end\" at a customer site by checking which proxy or middleware is buffering `http.response.body` events."},"check":{"q":"A streaming chat endpoint works locally but, behind a customer's gateway, tokens arrive only\nafter the full answer is complete, and abandoned chats keep running to completion. Using the ASGI\nmessage model, explain both symptoms and what you would check.","a":"Streaming is a sequence of `http.response.body` events with `more_body: True`; something between\nthe app and the browser is accumulating them until `more_body: False`. Candidates are a\n`BaseHTTPMiddleware` that reads the whole response, a compression layer, or the gateway buffering\nupstream responses; test by curling the app directly with `-N`, then each hop outward. Abandoned chats\ncontinue because a disconnect arrives as an `http.disconnect` event on `receive()`, not as an\nexception; if the handler never polls `request.is_disconnected()` or tries a send, it keeps awaiting\nthe provider. The fix is to check for disconnect between tokens, or run the provider call in a task\nthat is cancelled when the disconnect event arrives, and to confirm the gateway actually forwards the\nconnection close."},"quiz":[{"q":"When does Uvicorn start accepting HTTP connections for a FastAPI app with a lifespan handler?","options":["As soon as the process starts; startup runs concurrently with the first requests","After the app sends `lifespan.startup.complete`","After the first request triggers lazy initialisation","When the health endpoint first returns 200"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The lifespan handshake completes before the server begins serving on its socket, so a worker that is still loading a model is not serving yet, and a failed startup never receives traffic."},{"q":"How does an ASGI app learn that the client closed the connection mid-stream?","options":["An exception is raised at the next `await` in the handler","The server cancels the handler's task immediately","A subsequent `receive()` returns an `http.disconnect` event","The scope's `client` field becomes `None`"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Disconnect is a message, not an exception; the handler must read it, which is what `request.is_disconnected()` does, otherwise a long model call keeps running for nobody."},{"q":"With `uvicorn --workers 4`, how many times does the lifespan startup code run?","options":["Once, in the master process, with state shared by fork","Four times, once per worker process","Once per request until every worker is warm","Twice: once at bind time and once at first request"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Each worker is a separate process with its own event loop and app instance, so a model loaded in lifespan is loaded four times and occupies four times the memory."}]},{"key":"fastapi-for-ai/middleware-ordering","sources":["Official docs: Starlette","Official docs: FastAPI"],"why":"Cross-cutting concerns — request IDs, auth, CORS, compression, timing, tracing, body-size limits — do\nnot belong in every endpoint, so frameworks put them in a chain that every request passes through. The\nchain has to be ordered because the concerns depend on each other. A request-ID layer must run before\nanything that logs, or early log lines have no ID. CORS must wrap auth, or the browser receives a 401\nwith no `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` and reports an opaque \"CORS error\" instead of \"unauthorized\". A\nbody-size limit must run before anything reads the body. A tracing span should open before auth so\nrejected requests are still traced.\n\nThe naive approach, \"add them in whatever order and let each one be independent\", fails because\nmiddleware is not a set of independent filters; it is nested function calls. Each layer receives the\nrequest from the layer outside it and the response from the layer inside it. Headers added on the way\nout by an inner layer are visible to outer layers; values set on the way in by an outer layer are\nvisible to inner layers; nothing flows the other way. Exceptions propagate outward through the\nlayers, and a layer that returns early (a 401, a 413) short-circuits everything inside it. Ordering is\ntherefore semantics, not style.\n\nFastAPI adds a wrinkle: its `add_middleware` API inserts each new middleware on the outside, the\nopposite of what reading the file top to bottom suggests, and two framework-owned layers sit at fixed\npositions. Most \"my middleware does not see X\" bugs come from not knowing these two facts.","how":"### The stack is built once\nStarlette keeps `user_middleware` as a list. On the first request it builds the chain as\n`ServerErrorMiddleware(user_mw_0(user_mw_1(... ExceptionMiddleware(Router))))`: the first element of the\nlist is the outermost user layer. Because the stack is built lazily and then frozen, adding middleware\nafter the app has started raises an error in current Starlette; configure everything before the first\nrequest.\n\n### Two ways to add, two orderings\n- `app.add_middleware(M)` inserts `M` at index 0 of `user_middleware`. Each call pushes the new layer to\n  the *outside*. Reading a file with three `add_middleware` calls, the bottom one is the outermost; the\n  request hits it first.\n- `FastAPI(middleware=[Middleware(A), Middleware(B)])` keeps list order: A is outermost, B inside it.\n- `@app.middleware(\"http\")` is sugar for `add_middleware(BaseHTTPMiddleware, dispatch=fn)`, so\n  decorator-defined middleware follow the `add_middleware` rule: last defined, outermost.\n\n### The two fixed layers\n`ServerErrorMiddleware` is always the outermost layer. It catches any exception that escaped everything\nelse, returns a 500 (or the handler you registered for `500` / `Exception`), then re-raises so the\nserver logs it. *Because* it is outside your middleware, a 500 it generates never passes back through\n`CORSMiddleware`: a browser sees a CORS failure, not a 500. `ExceptionMiddleware` is always the\ninnermost layer, directly around the router. It converts `HTTPException` and anything with a registered\nhandler into a normal response. *Because* it is inside your middleware, your layers see a 404 or a 422\nas an ordinary response with a status code, not as an exception. Only unhandled exceptions travel\nthrough your middleware as exceptions, which is why a timing or logging middleware must use\n`try/finally` rather than code placed after `await call_next(request)`.\n\n### Request path and response path\nEach layer is either `async def __call__(self, scope, receive, send)` (pure ASGI) or\n`async def dispatch(self, request, call_next)` (`BaseHTTPMiddleware`). On the way in a layer may read or\nmutate the scope (set `scope[\"state\"][\"request_id\"]`), reject early, or wrap `receive` to count body\nbytes. On the way out it may wrap `send` to inject headers on `http.response.start`, time the response,\nor observe the status. A layer that returns before calling the next one short-circuits the rest.\n\n### Why pure ASGI middleware is preferred for streaming\n`BaseHTTPMiddleware` runs the downstream app in a separate task and exposes the response as a streaming\nbody. That is convenient but has costs: context variables set downstream are not visible to the\nmiddleware afterwards, the extra task adds overhead per request, and it has a history of interfering\nwith streaming and background tasks. For token streaming and trace propagation, write pure ASGI\nmiddleware that wraps `send`; it adds no task and passes `more_body` chunks through untouched.\n\n### A sane default order, outermost first\n1. Request ID / trace context, so every later log line and span has it.\n2. CORS, so every response, including auth rejections and preflight answers, gets its headers.\n3. Body-size limits and timeouts, to reject before spending anything.\n4. Authentication, which needs the trace and produces a principal for inner layers.\n5. Timing and metrics, inside auth if you want handler time, outside it if you want total time.\n6. Compression, kept away from token streams, since buffering for gzip delays each chunk.\nWith `add_middleware`, add these in *reverse*; with the `middleware=` list, in this order.","example":"The constructor-list form makes order explicit. A pure ASGI request-ID layer sits outermost, CORS wraps\neverything else, and a timing layer uses `try/finally` so exceptions are timed too. The 401 raised in\nthe endpoint is turned into a response by `ExceptionMiddleware`, then picks up CORS headers and the\nrequest ID on its way out.\n\n```python\nimport time, uuid\nfrom fastapi import FastAPI, HTTPException, Request\nfrom starlette.middleware import Middleware\nfrom starlette.middleware.cors import CORSMiddleware\nfrom starlette.middleware.base import BaseHTTPMiddleware\n\nclass RequestID:                                   # pure ASGI: no extra task, streaming-safe\n    def __init__(self, app): self.app = app\n    async def __call__(self, scope, receive, send):\n        if scope[\"type\"] != \"http\":\n            return await self.app(scope, receive, send)\n        rid = uuid.uuid4().hex[:12]\n        scope.setdefault(\"state\", {})[\"request_id\"] = rid\n        async def send_with_id(msg):\n            if msg[\"type\"] == \"http.response.start\":\n                msg = {**msg, \"headers\": [*msg.get(\"headers\", []), (b\"x-request-id\", rid.encode())]}\n            await send(msg)\n        await self.app(scope, receive, send_with_id)\n\nclass Timing(BaseHTTPMiddleware):\n    async def dispatch(self, request, call_next):\n        t0 = time.perf_counter()\n        try:\n            return await call_next(request)        # 4xx from HTTPException arrive here as responses\n        finally:                                   # unhandled exceptions pass through as exceptions\n            ms = (time.perf_counter() - t0) * 1000\n            print(request.state.request_id, request.url.path, f\"{ms:.1f}ms\")\n\napp = FastAPI(middleware=[                         # list order: first is outermost\n    Middleware(RequestID),\n    Middleware(CORSMiddleware, allow_origins=[\"https://app.example.com\"], allow_headers=[\"*\"]),\n    Middleware(Timing),\n])\n\n@app.post(\"/chat\")\nasync def chat(request: Request, body: dict):\n    if \"x-api-key\" not in request.headers:\n        raise HTTPException(401, \"missing key\")    # still gets CORS headers: CORS is outside\n    return {\"request_id\": request.state.request_id, \"reply\": \"...\"}\n```","tradeoffs":["Outer placement sees every request including ones later rejected, which is right for CORS and trace IDs but wrong for metrics meant to measure handler time; the choice flips with what the number is for.","`BaseHTTPMiddleware` is quick to write and gives you a Request/Response API, but the extra task per request, contextvar isolation and streaming interactions push you to pure ASGI once you stream tokens or propagate trace context.","Early rejection (size limits, auth) outermost saves work on bad requests, but anything outside the request-ID layer logs without an ID; so the ID layer stays outermost even though it does \"work\" for traffic that is about to be rejected.","Middleware is global per app; concerns needed on only some routes are cheaper as router-level dependencies, and the trade flips back when the concern must see raw ASGI events (body bytes, response chunks) that dependencies cannot."],"pitfalls":["Adding CORS before (hence inside) an auth middleware that returns 401 directly; symptom: the browser console shows a CORS error on failed logins and preflight OPTIONS requests fail, while curl works.","Measuring or logging after `await call_next(request)` without `try/finally`; symptom: requests that end in unhandled exceptions vanish from timing logs and metrics, so error latency is invisible.","Expecting CORS headers on 500 responses; `ServerErrorMiddleware` is outside everything, so its response skips `CORSMiddleware`. Symptom: the front end cannot read the error body of crashes; the fix is an exception handler that runs inside the stack.","Reading the body in a `BaseHTTPMiddleware` for logging and then expecting the endpoint to read it too; symptom: endpoints hang or receive an empty body only when request logging is enabled.","Assuming the file order of `add_middleware` calls is execution order; symptom: a request-ID middleware \"added first\" produces IDs that the auth middleware's log lines never contain."],"when":"Use middleware for concerns that must touch every request at the ASGI level: request IDs, CORS,\ncompression, body limits, tracing, global timing. Use dependencies instead when the concern is\nper-route, needs typed parameters, or should appear in OpenAPI (auth schemes). Decide order by writing\nthe request path as a list from the edge inwards and asking, for each layer, \"what must already exist\nwhen this runs, and who must see its output on the way back?\" Verify it empirically: a test that\ntriggers a 401 and asserts the CORS header, a test that raises in an endpoint and asserts the timing log\nline. In interviews, the questions are \"which middleware runs first with `add_middleware`?\" and \"why\ndoes my 500 have no CORS headers?\"; the mechanism above answers both.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Assembles the stack with the constructor list so order is explicit, writes pure ASGI middleware for streaming routes, and tests the 401-with-CORS case.","architect":"Owns which concerns are global middleware versus route dependencies versus the gateway, and where trace context is first created across services.","fde":"Untangles a customer's \"CORS error\" that is really an auth or crash problem, by tracing where in the stack the response was produced."},"check":{"q":"A team calls `add_middleware` three times in this order: `CORSMiddleware`, an API-key middleware\nthat returns 401 directly, then a `BaseHTTPMiddleware` that logs method, path, status and latency after\n`call_next`. Users report CORS errors on bad keys and the latency log never shows 500s. Explain both\nand fix the order.","a":"`add_middleware` inserts on the outside, so the logging layer is outermost, then API-key, then\nCORS innermost. A bad key is rejected by the API-key layer before the request reaches CORS, so the 401\nhas no `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` and the browser reports a CORS failure; preflight OPTIONS requests\nfail the same way because CORS never gets to answer them. The logger misses 500s because an unhandled\nexception propagates through `call_next` as an exception, skipping the code after it. Fix: add\nmiddleware in reverse of the desired outer-to-inner order (or use the `middleware=` list): logging\noutermost, CORS next, API-key inside CORS; and wrap `call_next` in `try/finally` so exceptions are\ntimed. Note that 500s produced by `ServerErrorMiddleware` still lack CORS headers unless you register\nan exception handler that runs inside the stack."},"quiz":[{"q":"With three `app.add_middleware()` calls A, B, C in that order, which runs first on an incoming request?","options":["A, because it was registered first","B, because Starlette balances the stack","C, because each call inserts at the outside of the stack","They run concurrently in separate tasks"],"answer":2,"explanation":"`add_middleware` inserts at index 0 of the user middleware list, and index 0 is the outermost layer, so the last added middleware sees the request first and the response last."},{"q":"Why does a 404 from `HTTPException` reach your middleware as a response, while a `KeyError` reaches it as an exception?","options":["Middleware only sees exceptions that subclass `Exception`","`ExceptionMiddleware` sits inside your middleware and converts handled exceptions to responses; unhandled ones propagate outward","`HTTPException` is raised inside a background task","The router catches all exceptions and returns them as JSON"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Starlette places `ExceptionMiddleware` innermost, so `HTTPException` and registered handlers produce responses before your layers see them; anything unhandled travels through your layers to the outermost `ServerErrorMiddleware`."},{"q":"Which placement guarantees that 401 responses from your auth layer carry CORS headers?","options":["Put auth outside CORS so it rejects before CORS does any work","Put CORS outside auth so the 401 passes back through it on the way out","Order does not matter because CORS headers are added by the browser","Register both with `@app.middleware(\"http\")` so they share a task"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Headers are added on the response path by layers outside the one that produced the response; CORS must therefore wrap auth, which also lets CORS answer preflight requests before auth rejects them."}]},{"key":"fastapi-for-ai/async-endpoints","sources":["Official docs: FastAPI","Official docs: AnyIO","Official docs: Starlette"],"why":"An AI endpoint spends almost all of its wall-clock time waiting. A chat completion call to a hosted\nmodel can take anywhere from two to sixty seconds; the Python work around it — validate the request,\nbuild messages, parse the response — takes about a millisecond. With one thread per request, serving\n300 concurrent chats means 300 threads that are asleep nearly all the time, each holding a stack and\ncompeting for the GIL during the tiny slices of work they do. Scaling that to thousands of users means\nmore processes, more memory and more money for CPU that sits idle.\n\nCooperative concurrency inverts this. A coroutine that hits `await client.post(...)` hands the loop\nback; the loop runs other coroutines until the socket has data. One process can keep thousands of such\nwaits in flight on a single thread, because waiting is free. That is the whole reason `async def`\nexists in a web framework, and for AI workloads the gain is unusually large because the wait-to-work\nratio is so extreme.\n\nThe naive version — \"write `async def` everywhere, it is the modern way\" — fails in the opposite\ndirection. The loop has exactly one thread. If a coroutine calls `requests.post`, `time.sleep`,\n`model.generate(...)` on a CPU or GPU, or a sync database driver, it holds the loop for the whole\nduration and nothing else in the process runs: not other requests, not health checks, not the\nheartbeat that keeps the load balancer happy. The symptom is a server that is fast at one request per\nsecond and collapses, every latency equal to the slowest call, at twenty. Knowing which code is\ncooperative and which is not is the real content of this topic.","how":"### Two execution paths\nWhen a request reaches a route, FastAPI resolves dependencies and calls the endpoint:\n- `async def` endpoints (and dependencies) are awaited directly on the event loop. They must only\n  `await` cooperative libraries.\n- `def` endpoints (and dependencies) are handed to `run_in_threadpool`, which uses AnyIO's\n  `to_thread.run_sync`. The loop keeps running while the thread blocks. AnyIO's default thread limiter\n  allows 40 concurrent threads per process, so the 41st blocking call queues behind the others; you can\n  raise it, at the cost of memory and GIL contention.\nPydantic validation and response serialisation happen on the loop in both cases; they are CPU work, but\nnormally sub-millisecond, which is acceptable *because* they are bounded, unlike a model forward pass.\n\n### What counts as blocking\nBlocking means the thread cannot be used for anything else until the call returns. In an AI service\nthe usual offenders are synchronous HTTP clients (`requests`, the sync variants of provider SDKs),\n`time.sleep`, synchronous database drivers, file I/O on large files, tokenising a very long document,\nand any local model inference (PyTorch, ONNX Runtime, llama.cpp bindings) whether on CPU or GPU, since\na GPU call blocks the calling thread until the result is back unless the library offers an async API.\nThe cooperative equivalents are `httpx.AsyncClient`, the `Async*` provider clients, async drivers\n(asyncpg, aiomysql), `aiofiles`, and `anyio.to_thread.run_sync` for anything that has no async form.\n\n### Fan-out and ordering\nBecause awaits yield, one request can run several provider calls concurrently with `asyncio.gather`\nor an AnyIO task group: embed three chunks at once, call two models and pick the better answer, or run\na guardrail classifier in parallel with the main completion. Latency becomes the maximum of the calls\ninstead of the sum. This is only possible on the async path; a `def` endpoint would need its own\nthreads to achieve the same overlap.\n\n### Threads and the GIL\n`def` endpoints parallelise waiting, not Python computation: two threads running pure-Python\ntokenisation share one interpreter lock. They do help for C and CUDA code that releases the GIL, which\nPyTorch and NumPy do for most heavy kernels, so a `def` endpoint running `model.generate()` lets the\nloop keep serving health checks while the GPU works. It does not make the GPU any less of a single\nshared resource; concurrency limits still apply.\n\n### Cancellation and disconnects\nOn the async path, a request's coroutine can be cancelled when the client disconnects (if your code\nchecks for it), which stops spending on a provider call nobody will read. A blocked thread cannot be\ncancelled; it runs to completion. That asymmetry is another reason to keep remote model calls on the\nasync path.\n\n### Choosing per endpoint\n- Calls a hosted LLM, embedding API, vector database, Redis, or Postgres over an async driver →\n  `async def`.\n- Runs a local model or a sync-only library you cannot change → `def`, or `async def` with\n  `await run_in_threadpool(fn, ...)` around just the blocking part.\n- Heavy local inference in production → neither; put the model behind a dedicated inference server and\n  go back to the first case.","example":"The commented-out version blocks the loop twice: a CPU reranker and a sync HTTP call. The live version\nruns the reranker in the thread pool, borrows the shared async client created in lifespan, and fans\nout the completion and a safety classifier so the request's latency is the slower of the two, not\ntheir sum.\n\n```python\nimport anyio, httpx\nfrom fastapi import FastAPI, Request\nfrom fastapi.concurrency import run_in_threadpool\nfrom pydantic import BaseModel\n\napp = FastAPI()\n\nclass Ask(BaseModel):\n    question: str\n    candidates: list[str]\n\ndef rerank_sync(question: str, docs: list[str]) -> list[str]:\n    # stand-in for a CPU/GPU cross-encoder; blocks for the duration of the forward pass\n    return sorted(docs, key=lambda d: -len(set(d.split()) & set(question.split())))\n\n# BAD: both calls hold the event loop; every other request in the process waits.\n# @app.post(\"/ask\")\n# async def ask_blocking(body: Ask):\n#     ranked = rerank_sync(body.question, body.candidates)\n#     r = httpx.post(\"https://api.example.com/v1/complete\", json={\"q\": body.question, \"ctx\": ranked[:3]})\n#     return r.json()\n\n@app.post(\"/ask\")\nasync def ask(body: Ask, request: Request):\n    client: httpx.AsyncClient = request.app.state.http       # created once in lifespan, with base_url\n    ranked = await run_in_threadpool(rerank_sync, body.question, body.candidates)\n    results = {}\n    async def call(name, path, payload):\n        r = await client.post(path, json=payload, timeout=30)\n        results[name] = r.json()\n    async with anyio.create_task_group() as tg:               # fan out: completion + guardrail\n        tg.start_soon(call, \"answer\", \"/v1/complete\", {\"q\": body.question, \"ctx\": ranked[:3]})\n        tg.start_soon(call, \"safety\", \"/v1/classify\", {\"text\": body.question})\n    if results[\"safety\"].get(\"flagged\"):\n        return {\"answer\": None, \"reason\": \"blocked\"}\n    return {\"answer\": results[\"answer\"], \"sources\": ranked[:3]}\n```","tradeoffs":["`async def` gives huge concurrency per process for I/O-bound work, but one accidental blocking call degrades every request; the win flips to a loss exactly when a sync library sneaks in.","`def` endpoints are safe by default and tolerate sync libraries, but each in-flight request occupies a thread from a bounded pool (40 by default), so throughput caps far lower under long calls.","Fan-out with `gather` or task groups cuts latency to the slowest call, but multiplies provider concurrency and cost, and needs per-request concurrency limits once traffic grows.","Running blocking inference in the thread pool keeps the loop alive, yet threads cannot be cancelled on disconnect and still contend for the GIL on the pure-Python parts."],"pitfalls":["Using a sync provider SDK (`OpenAI()` rather than `AsyncOpenAI()`, or `requests`) inside `async def`; symptom: p50 latency fine in isolation, every latency equal to the slowest call under modest load, health checks timing out.","Calling `time.sleep` for retry back-off inside a coroutine; symptom: a single rate-limited request pauses the whole process for the back-off period.","Declaring dependencies `async def` and doing blocking work in them (sync DB session creation, sync JWKS fetch); symptom: the same stall, but harder to find because it is outside the endpoint body.","Running a GPU model directly in `async def` to \"keep it async\"; symptom: throughput equals one request at a time with no overlap of network waits, and the loop-lag metric spikes to the model's latency.","Raising the thread limiter to hundreds to fix slow `def` endpoints; symptom: memory growth and GIL thrash with no throughput gain for Python-bound code."],"when":"Default to `async def` for any endpoint whose time goes to remote calls, which is most AI gateway, RAG\nand agent endpoints, and make sure every library on that path is actually async. Use `def` or\n`run_in_threadpool` for unavoidable blocking work, and bound how much of it can run at once. Measure\nevent-loop lag (schedule a no-op every second and record how late it runs) and the thread-pool queue\ndepth; lag above a few milliseconds under load means something is blocking. Day to day this shows up\nas a code-review comment on imports: `import requests` in an async service is a bug until proven\notherwise. In interviews, expect \"what happens if you call a sync function in an async endpoint?\" and\n\"when would you use `def` instead?\"; the answers are the stall and the thread pool.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Picks the async client of every SDK, wraps leftover sync calls in `run_in_threadpool`, and adds an event-loop-lag gauge to catch regressions before users do.","architect":"Decides that local inference lives behind a model server rather than inside the API process, so API workers stay purely I/O-bound and cheap to scale.","fde":"Finds the one synchronous call a customer's team added to an async endpoint when they report \"it falls over at thirty users\"."},"check":{"q":"A RAG endpoint is `async def`: it embeds the query with a local sentence-transformer (about\n40 ms on CPU), queries a vector database over an async client, then calls a hosted LLM asynchronously.\nUnder load, throughput plateaus at roughly 25 requests per second even though the LLM provider is\nnowhere near its limits. Explain and fix.","a":"The embedding call is synchronous CPU work executed on the event loop. At 40 ms per call the\nloop can do at most about 25 such calls per second, and while each one runs no other coroutine\nprogresses, so the asynchronous vector-database and LLM calls never overlap as they should. Moving the\nembedding to `await run_in_threadpool(embed, query)` frees the loop; the thread pool then bounds\nembedding concurrency and NumPy or PyTorch release the GIL during the heavy kernels. Beyond that,\nbatch embeddings across concurrent requests or move the embedder to a separate service, so the API\nprocess is purely I/O-bound and its throughput is set by provider latency and connection limits rather\nthan by CPU time spent on the loop."},"quiz":[{"q":"What happens when an `async def` endpoint calls a synchronous HTTP client that takes 5 seconds?","options":["FastAPI detects the blocking call and moves it to a thread","The event loop is blocked for 5 seconds, so every other request in the process waits","Only that request is delayed; others proceed on other loop threads","The call is cancelled after the default timeout"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The loop runs on one thread and cannot switch coroutines while a non-awaiting call holds it; concurrency on the async path depends entirely on code yielding with `await`."},{"q":"Why can a plain `def` endpoint running `model.generate()` keep the server responsive while an `async def` one cannot?","options":["`def` endpoints are compiled to C and release the GIL","`def` endpoints run in a separate process","`def` endpoints run in a thread pool, so the loop thread stays free to serve other requests","`async def` endpoints are limited to one request at a time"],"answer":2,"explanation":"FastAPI dispatches sync endpoints through `run_in_threadpool`; the loop continues scheduling other coroutines while the worker thread blocks, bounded by the thread limiter's capacity."},{"q":"When does fanning out two provider calls with `asyncio.gather` reduce latency?","options":["Always, because gather parallelises CPU work across cores","When both calls spend their time awaiting I/O, so their waits overlap on one loop","Only when the endpoint is declared with `def`","Never; gather only orders the results"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Overlap comes from both coroutines yielding while sockets are idle; CPU-bound work does not overlap on a single loop thread, and the gain is capped by the slower of the two calls."}]},{"key":"fastapi-for-ai/pydantic-request-response-models","sources":["Official docs: FastAPI","Official docs: Pydantic"],"why":"An AI endpoint takes rich, nested input — a list of messages with roles, optional tool definitions,\nsampling parameters, tenant metadata — and returns rich output. If the handler reads a raw dict, every\nfield needs a hand-written check: is `messages` a list, does each item have `role` and `content`, is\n`temperature` a number between 0 and 2, is `max_tokens` an integer. Teams skip those checks, and the\nfailures arrive late and expensively: the provider rejects the call after a network round trip, or\naccepts a nonsense value and returns garbage, or a typo like `\"temprature\"` silently falls back to the\ndefault and nobody notices for a month. Output is worse: handlers return whatever dict they built, so\na refactor that adds `raw_provider_response` or `cost_usd` to an internal object leaks straight to the\ncustomer.\n\nHand-rolled validation fails because it is unenforced, undocumented and inconsistent across endpoints:\nthree routes validate `messages` three ways, the OpenAPI document says nothing about any of them, and\nclient teams discover the rules by trial. A separate schema file enforces shape but lives outside the\ncode and drifts from it.\n\nPydantic models are Python classes, so the contract lives next to the handler, carries types the IDE\nand mypy understand, and generates JSON Schema for OpenAPI automatically. FastAPI runs validation\nbefore the handler is called, *because* the handler should only ever see data that already satisfies\nthe contract, and runs response validation after it, *because* the output contract must hold\nregardless of which code path produced the data.","how":"### Inbound: body → model → handler\n1. FastAPI reads the ASGI body events, parses JSON and, for each parameter annotated with a `BaseModel`\n   subclass, calls Pydantic validation on the parsed data. Query, path and header parameters are\n   validated the same way from their own sources.\n2. Validation coerces and checks: `\"0.7\"` becomes `0.7` for a `float` field under Pydantic's default\n   lax mode, missing required fields and wrong types produce errors, `Field(ge=0, le=2)` enforces\n   ranges, `Literal[\"user\", \"assistant\", \"system\"]` restricts enumerations, `max_length` bounds lists\n   and strings, and `@field_validator` / `@model_validator` express cross-field rules such as \"either\n   `prompt` or `messages`, not both\".\n3. On failure FastAPI raises `RequestValidationError` and returns HTTP 422 with a `detail` list: each\n   entry has `loc` (the path to the field, such as `[\"body\", \"messages\", 1, \"role\"]`), `msg` and `type`.\n   Clients can fix the exact field without reading server logs.\n4. On success the handler receives a typed model instance, not a dict.\n\nExtra fields are ignored by default. For AI parameters that is dangerous — a misspelt `max_tokens`\nsilently disappears — so request models usually set `model_config = ConfigDict(extra=\"forbid\")`,\nturning typos into 422s with an `extra_forbidden` error.\n\n### Outbound: return value → response model → JSON\nWhen a route declares a response model (via `response_model=` or the return annotation), the returned\nobject is validated against it and serialised through it: only declared fields are emitted, alias and\n`exclude_none` options apply, and a return value that does not fit raises a server-side validation\nerror instead of shipping a wrong shape. The response model is also what OpenAPI shows clients. The\n`response_model` lesson covers the options in detail; this one is about designing the pair.\n\n### Design rules that matter for AI APIs\n- **Separate models per direction.** `ChatRequest` and `ChatResponse` evolve independently; sharing one\n  class forces every field to be optional and leaks internals. Internal objects (provider payloads,\n  DB rows) get their own models that never reach a route signature.\n- **Discriminated unions for message content.** A `content` that is either text or a list of parts\n  (text, image URL, tool result) is a `Union` with `Field(discriminator=\"type\")`, so validation picks\n  the branch from the `type` field and errors point at the right branch instead of listing all of them.\n- **Bound everything that costs money.** `max_tokens: int = Field(default=1024, ge=1, le=8192)`,\n  `messages: list[Message] = Field(min_length=1, max_length=200)`, and a model validator that caps\n  total characters, so prompt-size limits are enforced at the contract rather than discovered as\n  provider errors or surprise bills.\n- **Version by model, not by flag.** A breaking change gets a new request model on a new route;\n  additive optional fields with defaults are backward compatible.\n- **Strict where coercion would hide bugs.** Pydantic's strict mode (`ConfigDict(strict=True)` or\n  `Strict*` types) stops `\"1\"` being accepted as `1` when a client sending strings is itself the bug.\n\n### Why validation lives in the framework and not the handler\nRunning it before the handler means dependencies that need the body (a token-budget check, a tenant\nquota) see validated data too; running response validation in the framework means a code path cannot\nforget it. Pydantic's validation core is compiled, so for typical chat payloads validation costs\nwell under a millisecond and is negligible next to a model call.","example":"A chat endpoint with a forbidding request model, bounded sampling parameters, a discriminated content\nunion, a cross-field rule and a separate response model that exposes usage but not cost. Sending\n`{\"messages\": [{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"hi\"}], \"temprature\": 0.1}` returns a 422 whose `loc` is\n`[\"body\", \"temprature\"]` and whose `type` is `extra_forbidden`.\n\n```python\nfrom typing import Annotated, Literal, Union\nfrom fastapi import FastAPI\nfrom pydantic import BaseModel, ConfigDict, Field, model_validator\n\nclass TextPart(BaseModel):\n    type: Literal[\"text\"]\n    text: str = Field(max_length=50_000)\n\nclass ImagePart(BaseModel):\n    type: Literal[\"image_url\"]\n    url: str\n\nPart = Annotated[Union[TextPart, ImagePart], Field(discriminator=\"type\")]\n\nclass Message(BaseModel):\n    model_config = ConfigDict(extra=\"forbid\")\n    role: Literal[\"system\", \"user\", \"assistant\"]\n    content: Union[str, list[Part]]\n\nclass ChatRequest(BaseModel):\n    model_config = ConfigDict(extra=\"forbid\")          # \"temprature\" -> 422, not silently ignored\n    messages: list[Message] = Field(min_length=1, max_length=200)\n    temperature: float = Field(default=0.7, ge=0, le=2)\n    max_tokens: int = Field(default=1024, ge=1, le=8192)\n    stream: bool = False\n\n    @model_validator(mode=\"after\")\n    def first_message_not_assistant(self):\n        if self.messages[0].role == \"assistant\":\n            raise ValueError(\"conversation must start with a system or user message\")\n        return self\n\nclass Usage(BaseModel):\n    prompt_tokens: int\n    completion_tokens: int\n\nclass ChatResponse(BaseModel):\n    id: str\n    content: str\n    usage: Usage                                         # cost_usd and provider payload stay internal\n\napp = FastAPI()\n\n@app.post(\"/v1/chat\", response_model=ChatResponse)\nasync def chat(req: ChatRequest) -> ChatResponse:\n    # ... call the model here; stubbed ...\n    return ChatResponse(id=\"msg_1\", content=\"hi\", usage=Usage(prompt_tokens=12, completion_tokens=1))\n```","tradeoffs":["Strict contracts catch client errors early and document themselves, but every provider feature you want to pass through needs a model change; the trade flips toward a permissive pass-through field when you proxy a fast-moving provider API.","`extra=\"forbid\"` turns typos into 422s, which protects money and correctness, but makes additive client changes fail against older servers; relax it only on fields explicitly designed as open bags.","Validation on every request and response costs CPU on the loop; negligible for chat payloads, it flips to noticeable for responses carrying thousands of embedding vectors, where returning a raw `Response` can be justified.","Separate request, response and internal models triple the class count, but a single shared model couples the API to storage and leaks fields the moment someone adds one."],"pitfalls":["Leaving `extra` at its default and relying on clients to spell parameters correctly; symptom: a parameter \"has no effect\" for weeks because it never reached the provider.","Using one model for the DB row, the request and the response; symptom: a new internal column (`cost_usd`, `system_prompt`) appears in API output after an unrelated change.","Returning the provider's raw response dict as your response; symptom: your API's shape changes when the provider's does, and provider metadata reaches customers.","Unbounded `max_tokens` and message lists; symptom: a single request produces an outsized provider bill or a timeout, and the 422 that should have stopped it never fires.","Catching `RequestValidationError` and returning a generic \"bad request\"; symptom: client teams cannot tell which field was wrong and support tickets replace self-service fixes."],"when":"Every JSON endpoint gets a request model and a response model; the question is how strict and how\nmany. Use `extra=\"forbid\"` and ranged `Field`s for anything that controls model behaviour or cost. Use\ndiscriminated unions when a field can take several shapes. Use separate models for input, output and\ninternal state from day one, because splitting them later is a breaking change. Watch the 422 rate per\nfield as a product metric: a spike after a client release points at a schema mismatch. In interviews,\nbe ready to design the request model for a chat endpoint out loud, including bounds and the 422\npayload, and to explain why the response model is not the same class.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes the models with bounds and validators, keeps internal and external models apart, and reads 422 `loc` paths to debug client integrations quickly.","architect":"Treats request and response models as the public API contract, owning its versioning policy and which provider features are exposed or hidden.","fde":"Maps a customer's existing message format onto the request model with a discriminated union or an adapter, and uses the 422 output to speed up their integration."},"check":{"q":"Your chat endpoint proxies to a hosted model. A customer complains that setting `\"top_p\": 0.1`\nchanges nothing, and separately a security review finds the `system_prompt` your service injects\nappears in API responses. Diagnose both using request and response models.","a":"The `top_p` symptom means the request model does not declare the field and allows extra fields\nby default, so Pydantic discards it silently and the provider never sees it; either add\n`top_p: float = Field(ge=0, le=1)` and forward it, or set `extra=\"forbid\"` so unknown fields return a\n422 that tells the customer the parameter is unsupported. The leak means the response model is either\nmissing or the same class as the internal object that carries the injected prompt; define a dedicated\n`ChatResponse` with only the public fields and declare it on the route, so FastAPI filters the output\nand OpenAPI documents exactly what clients receive. Add a contract test asserting `system_prompt` is\nabsent from the serialised response."},"quiz":[{"q":"A client sends `{\"messages\": [...], \"temprature\": 0.2}` to an endpoint whose request model does not set `extra`. What happens?","options":["A 422 lists `temprature` as an unknown field","The request succeeds and the misspelt field is silently dropped","The value is assigned to `temperature` by fuzzy matching","The handler receives it in a `model_extra` dict and must handle it"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Pydantic's default is to ignore extra fields, so the typo disappears; `ConfigDict(extra=\"forbid\")` makes it a 422 with an `extra_forbidden` error pointing at the field."},{"q":"Why should the response model be a different class from the request model and from internal objects?","options":["Pydantic cannot validate the same class in both directions","So output fields are an explicit allow-list and internal additions cannot leak to clients","Because FastAPI requires unique class names per route","To reduce JSON size by removing type information"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Response filtering only protects you if the response model lists exactly the public fields; reusing an internal class means every new internal attribute becomes public by default."},{"q":"What does the `loc` entry `[\"body\", \"messages\", 1, \"role\"]` in a 422 response tell the client?","options":["The request had one too many messages","The second message is missing entirely","The `role` field of the second message (index 1) failed validation","The body must be sent as form data, not JSON"],"answer":2,"explanation":"`loc` is the path from the request source down to the offending value, so clients can fix the precise field instead of guessing from a generic error message."}]},{"key":"fastapi-for-ai/response-model","sources":["Official docs: FastAPI","Official docs: Pydantic"],"why":"A handler's return value is whatever the last line of code built. Over time that drifts: someone\nattaches the raw provider response for debugging, an ORM object grows a `cost_usd` column, a dict picks\nup a `debug_prompt` key behind a feature flag. Without an output contract, each of these ships to every\nclient the day it is merged, and the OpenAPI document — generated from nothing — describes an empty\nobject. Clients write code against the accidental shape, and the accidental shape becomes the API you\ncan never change.\n\nThe naive alternative, \"be careful about what you return\", has no enforcement: the leak happens in a\ncode path the reviewer did not read, or in an exception branch that returns a different dict. A second\nnaive alternative, \"construct the output model by hand in every handler\", works until one handler\nreturns the ORM object directly because it has the same fields, and now the ORM object's next\nattribute is public too.\n\n`response_model` moves the contract from the handler to the route declaration, where it applies to\nevery return path. Validation catches a shape bug on the server, as a 500 with a clear error in your\nlogs, rather than in a client's parser. Filtering turns the model into an allow-list: the handler can\nreturn a superset — a dict, an ORM row, an internal object — and only declared fields are serialised.\nAnd because the same declaration feeds OpenAPI, documentation cannot drift from behaviour. The\nreasoning mirrors request models: the framework enforces the contract *because* handlers will not do\nso reliably.","how":"### Declaring it\nEither `@app.get(\"/x\", response_model=Item)` or a return annotation `async def f() -> Item:`; recent\nFastAPI versions treat the annotation as the response model, and the explicit parameter wins when both\nare present. `response_model=None` turns the machinery off, which you need when the annotation is a\n`Response` subclass or a type the validator cannot build a field for.\n\n### What happens to the return value\n1. The handler returns something: a model instance, a dict, an ORM object, a list.\n2. If it is already a `Response` (including `StreamingResponse`, `JSONResponse`, `RedirectResponse`),\n   FastAPI sends it as-is. No validation, no filtering, no response-model serialisation, *because* you\n   have taken over the wire format; streaming token endpoints therefore get no protection from\n   `response_model`.\n3. Otherwise the value is validated against the response model. A dict or object with extra keys is\n   accepted and the extras dropped; a missing required field or wrong type raises\n   `ResponseValidationError`, which surfaces as a 500 so the bug is visible to you rather than shipped\n   silently as a wrong shape.\n4. The validated data is serialised with the model's rules: aliases when `response_model_by_alias` is\n   true (the default), and `response_model_include`, `response_model_exclude`,\n   `response_model_exclude_unset`, `response_model_exclude_defaults` and `response_model_exclude_none`\n   control which fields appear.\n5. The result goes through the route's `response_class` (`JSONResponse` by default; `ORJSONResponse`\n   for speed) to bytes.\n\n### The filtering semantics that matter\n- Undeclared fields on the returned object are dropped. This is the allow-list behaviour and the\n  security property.\n- `response_model_exclude_unset=True` emits only fields the handler explicitly set, so model defaults\n  do not appear; useful for partial responses, dangerous if clients rely on defaults being present.\n- `response_model_exclude_none=True` drops `None` values; optional fields disappear rather than appear\n  as `null`, which changes what clients must handle.\n- Unions: `response_model=Union[Chunk, Done]` validates against the branches; a discriminated union\n  makes the choice deterministic and the OpenAPI `oneOf` readable.\n- Lists: `response_model=list[Embedding]` validates every element, which is where the cost shows up for\n  large payloads.\n\n### Interaction with OpenAPI\nThe response model becomes the `200` response schema in the generated document, so SDK generators and\ncontract tests work from the same source as the server. Other status codes are declared with\n`responses={404: {\"model\": ErrorOut}}`.\n\n### Cost\nValidating and re-serialising the output is extra CPU on the event loop, proportional to payload size.\nFor chat responses it is negligible. For an endpoint returning thousands of embedding vectors it is\nmeasurable, and returning a pre-built `ORJSONResponse`, giving up validation on that route, is a\nlegitimate choice once you have measured it.","example":"An internal model with fields that must never leave the process, a public model, and a handler that\nreturns the internal object unchanged. The declaration does the filtering; `finish_reason` is omitted\nwhen `None` because of `response_model_exclude_none`. Returning an internal object with `text=None`\nwould raise `ResponseValidationError` and produce a 500 rather than a malformed success.\n\n```python\nfrom typing import Annotated, Optional\nfrom fastapi import Body, FastAPI\nfrom pydantic import BaseModel\n\napp = FastAPI()\n\nclass CompletionInternal(BaseModel):          # what the service works with\n    id: str\n    text: str\n    model: str\n    finish_reason: Optional[str] = None\n    cost_usd: float                           # never public\n    provider_raw: dict                        # never public\n\nclass CompletionOut(BaseModel):               # what clients receive\n    id: str\n    text: str\n    model: str\n    finish_reason: Optional[str] = None\n\ndef run_model(prompt: str) -> CompletionInternal:\n    return CompletionInternal(id=\"cmpl_1\", text=prompt[::-1], model=\"demo-1\", cost_usd=0.0003,\n                              provider_raw={\"headers\": {\"x-ratelimit-remaining\": \"99\"}})\n\n@app.post(\"/complete\", response_model=CompletionOut, response_model_exclude_none=True)\nasync def complete(prompt: Annotated[str, Body(embed=True)]) -> CompletionOut:\n    internal = run_model(prompt)\n    return internal        # superset is fine: cost_usd and provider_raw are dropped on the way out\n\n# GET /openapi.json shows CompletionOut as the 200 schema; cost_usd and provider_raw do not appear.\n```","tradeoffs":["Filtering gives a hard allow-list on output, but it also silently drops fields you forgot to declare; the protection flips into a debugging trap when a handler \"returns the right thing\" and the client never sees it.","Validation surfaces shape bugs as 500s instead of malformed success responses, which is right for correctness, but means a benign internal change can take a route down until the model is updated.","Declaring the model in the return annotation keeps code and contract together, yet an annotation that is not a valid Pydantic type (a `Response` subclass, an awkward union) needs `response_model=None` or an explicit model.","Re-serialising through the model costs CPU per byte of output; negligible for chat, measurable for bulk embeddings, where a raw `ORJSONResponse` trades the guarantee for speed."],"pitfalls":["Returning a `StreamingResponse` or `JSONResponse` and believing `response_model` still filters it; symptom: internal fields appear only on the streaming route, and OpenAPI claims a schema the route does not enforce.","Using `response_model_exclude_unset` on a model whose defaults clients depend on; symptom: fields \"randomly\" missing from responses depending on which code path set them.","Reusing the request model as the response model to save a class; symptom: clients must send fields that only make sense in output, and output exposes fields that only make sense in input.","Treating a `ResponseValidationError` 500 as a library bug and disabling response validation; symptom: a handler branch returns the wrong shape and ships for months.","Annotating a handler `-> dict` or `-> Any`; symptom: the contract silently loosens and filtering stops, without anyone having touched `response_model=`."],"when":"Declare a response model on every JSON route, separate from the request model and from internal\nobjects, and make it the only place output shape is defined. Turn it off deliberately, with\n`response_model=None` and a comment, for routes that return a `Response` (streaming tokens, file\ndownloads) and for bulk routes where measurement shows serialisation cost matters; those routes need\nanother output check, such as a contract test. Review `response_model_exclude_*` options as API\ndecisions, not conveniences, since each changes what clients must handle. In interviews the classic\nquestion is \"what does `response_model` do that a return type does not?\", and the answer is\nvalidation, filtering and documentation on every return path, plus the `Response` escape hatch.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Declares `response_model` per route, keeps an `*Out` model per resource, and investigates `ResponseValidationError` logs as shape bugs rather than silencing them.","architect":"Treats the set of response models as the versioned public contract and decides where filtering is relaxed for throughput and how that gap is covered.","fde":"Shows a customer, via `/openapi.json`, exactly which fields a route returns, and uses `response_model` to hide the customer's own internal metadata from downstream consumers."},"check":{"q":"A team's `/complete` route returns an ORM object; the model it is declared with has `id`, `text`\nand `model`. A new column `prompt_hash` is added to the ORM table, and separately the team adds a\n`/complete/stream` route returning a `StreamingResponse` with the same `response_model`. What is and is\nnot protected, and why?","a":"On `/complete` the new column is not exposed: FastAPI validates the ORM object against the\ndeclared model and serialises only `id`, `text` and `model`, so `prompt_hash` is filtered as an\nundeclared field; the declaration acts as an allow-list on every return path. On `/complete/stream`\nnothing is protected: a `StreamingResponse` is a `Response`, and FastAPI sends `Response` objects as-is\nwithout validation or filtering, so whatever the generator yields, including `prompt_hash` if the\nchunks are built from the ORM row, reaches the client; the `response_model` there only affects OpenAPI\nand misleads readers. The fix is to build stream chunks from an explicit chunk model, set\n`response_model=None` with a comment on the streaming route, and cover it with a contract test."},"quiz":[{"q":"A handler declared with `response_model=Out` returns a dict containing every `Out` field plus `cost_usd`. What does the client receive?","options":["A 500, because extra fields fail validation","A JSON object with only the `Out` fields; `cost_usd` is dropped","All fields, because dicts bypass the response model","The `Out` fields plus `cost_usd` under an `extra` key"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Response-model serialisation is an allow-list: declared fields are validated and emitted, undeclared ones are discarded, which is the security property the mechanism exists for."},{"q":"Which return value is sent to the client without any `response_model` validation or filtering?","options":["A Pydantic model instance of a different class","A plain dict","A `Response` object such as `StreamingResponse` or `JSONResponse`","A list of Pydantic models"],"answer":2,"explanation":"FastAPI treats a returned `Response` as the final wire format and does not touch it; that is the escape hatch for streaming and custom encodings, and also the point where the contract stops applying."},{"q":"What is the effect of `response_model_exclude_unset=True`?","options":["Fields with `None` values are omitted","Only fields the handler explicitly set are emitted; model defaults are left out","Required fields may be omitted without a validation error","The response skips validation to save CPU"],"answer":1,"explanation":"`exclude_unset` serialises just the fields that were set on the returned object, which suits partial responses but surprises clients that expect default-valued fields to be present."}]},{"key":"fastapi-for-ai/dependency-injection","sources":["Official docs: FastAPI"],"why":"Every endpoint in an AI service needs the same things: who is calling, which tenant they belong to, a\nclient for the model provider, a retrieval index, a database session, a request ID for logs. The first\nversion of the code gets these from module-level globals and helper calls at the top of each handler.\nThat fails in three predictable ways. Tests cannot replace the globals cleanly, so unit tests hit the\nreal provider or the suite monkeypatches half the module. Auth and quota checks are duplicated and one\nroute forgets them. And resources with a lifecycle — a session that must be closed, a transaction that\nmust be committed or rolled back — are cleaned up inconsistently, usually with a `try/finally` that one\nhandler lacks.\n\n\"Write a decorator\" fixes duplication but not typing or testing, and decorators cannot easily pass\nvalues into the handler. \"Use a DI container library\" works but adds a second configuration language\nand runs outside the framework's request model, so it knows nothing about request scope, OpenAPI\nsecurity schemes or the thread pool.\n\nFastAPI's `Depends` is function-level DI that is part of the request pipeline. Dependencies are\nordinary callables with their own typed parameters, which can themselves be dependencies, so the\nframework builds a tree per route and resolves it per request *because* the request is the natural\nunit of scope for auth, sessions and tracing. Generator dependencies (`yield`) attach cleanup to the\nsame tree *because* teardown must run even when the handler raises. `dependency_overrides` exists\n*because* tests need a seam to replace the model client without touching production code. Resolved\nvalues appear in OpenAPI when they carry security metadata, so the documentation follows the code.","how":"### Declaring and resolving\nA dependency is any callable: a function, an `async def`, a class, or an object with `__call__`. The\nendpoint declares `client: Annotated[LLMClient, Depends(get_llm)]`. At request time FastAPI inspects\nthe signature, resolves `get_llm`'s own parameters (path, query, header and body parameters, or further\n`Depends`), calls it, and passes the result in. Resolution is a depth-first walk of the tree, leaves\nfirst, so an endpoint depending on `get_user`, which depends on `get_token`, which reads the\n`Authorization` header, receives a fully built user object.\n\n### Per-request caching\nIf the same dependency appears more than once in one request's tree — `get_settings` under both\n`get_llm` and `get_retriever` — it is called once and the value reused, *because* most dependencies are\ndeterministic for a request and calling them twice would double DB lookups or token verifications.\n`Depends(fn, use_cache=False)` opts out for dependencies whose repeated evaluation is the point (a\nfresh timestamp, a new sub-span).\n\n### Sync and async\n`async def` dependencies run on the event loop; `def` dependencies run in the thread pool, exactly like\nendpoints. A dependency that does blocking work (verifying a JWT with a sync library, opening a sync DB\nsession) should be `def`, or it stalls the loop for every request in the process.\n\n### Cleanup with yield\nA dependency written as a generator runs up to `yield`, hands the value to the handler, and resumes\nafter the handler finishes. Exit code runs in reverse order of setup, and an exception raised in the\nendpoint is re-raised at the `yield` point, so `try/except/finally` around it can roll back a\ntransaction and still close the session. Exactly when the exit code runs relative to sending the\nresponse bytes has changed between FastAPI versions, so never use a yielded resource from a background\ntask and never rely on teardown having finished before the client sees the response.\n\n### Side-effect dependencies\n`dependencies=[Depends(verify_api_key)]` on a route, a router or the whole app runs the dependency\nwithout binding its return value, which is how you enforce auth or quotas on an entire router without\nthreading a parameter through every handler.\n\n### Security dependencies\n`Security(get_user, scopes=[\"chat:write\"])` is `Depends` plus scope metadata; combined with\n`OAuth2PasswordBearer`, `APIKeyHeader` or `HTTPBearer` it also emits the security scheme into OpenAPI,\nso the docs page shows the lock icon and clients know what to send.\n\n### Overrides\n`app.dependency_overrides[get_llm] = lambda: FakeLLM()` replaces a dependency everywhere it is used, for\nas long as the entry exists. Because dependencies are identified by the callable object, the override\nreaches nested uses too. This is the seam for running an AI service's tests against a canned model, a\nSQLite session or a fixed tenant.\n\n### Where dependencies meet lifespan\nDependencies do not create expensive resources; they *fetch* them. The provider client, model, engine\nor index is created once in lifespan and stored on `app.state`; a dependency reads\n`request.app.state.llm` and returns it, so every handler gets the shared object through the same typed,\noverridable interface. Request-scoped objects (a session, a per-tenant configuration, a trace span)\nare created inside the dependency and torn down after `yield`.","example":"An API-key dependency, a shared LLM client fetched from `app.state`, a yield dependency holding a\nper-request budget that is recorded even when the handler raises, and the test override that keeps\nthe suite from spending tokens. `get_tenant` appears twice in the tree (directly and under `budget`)\nbut runs once per request.\n\n```python\nfrom typing import Annotated\nfrom fastapi import Depends, FastAPI, Header, HTTPException, Request\nfrom fastapi.testclient import TestClient\n\napp = FastAPI()\nTENANTS = {\"key-acme\": {\"id\": \"acme\", \"daily_budget_usd\": 50.0}}\n\ndef get_tenant(x_api_key: Annotated[str, Header()]) -> dict:\n    tenant = TENANTS.get(x_api_key)\n    if tenant is None:\n        raise HTTPException(401, \"invalid api key\")\n    return tenant\n\ndef get_llm(request: Request):\n    return request.app.state.llm       # created once in lifespan (not shown); fetched per request\n\nclass Budget:\n    def __init__(self, limit): self.limit, self.spent = limit, 0.0\n    def charge(self, usd):\n        self.spent += usd\n        if self.spent > self.limit: raise HTTPException(429, \"budget exceeded\")\n\nasync def budget(tenant: Annotated[dict, Depends(get_tenant)]):\n    b = Budget(tenant[\"daily_budget_usd\"])\n    try:\n        yield b\n    finally:\n        print(\"record spend\", tenant[\"id\"], b.spent)   # runs even if the handler raised\n\n@app.post(\"/chat\")\nasync def chat(prompt: str, tenant: Annotated[dict, Depends(get_tenant)],\n               b: Annotated[Budget, Depends(budget)], llm=Depends(get_llm)):\n    text, cost = await llm.complete(prompt)\n    b.charge(cost)\n    return {\"tenant\": tenant[\"id\"], \"text\": text}\n\nclass FakeLLM:                                       # test seam: no production code changes\n    async def complete(self, prompt): return f\"echo: {prompt}\", 0.001\napp.dependency_overrides[get_llm] = lambda: FakeLLM()\nwith TestClient(app) as c:\n    r = c.post(\"/chat\", params={\"prompt\": \"hi\"}, headers={\"x-api-key\": \"key-acme\"})\n    assert r.json()[\"text\"] == \"echo: hi\"\n```","tradeoffs":["Typed, per-request resolution makes every handler's needs explicit and testable, but deep dependency trees hide the cost of each request (three DB lookups behind one parameter) until you trace them.","Per-request caching avoids repeated work within a request and flips into a bug when a dependency is meant to produce fresh values each call, which is what `use_cache=False` is for.","`dependency_overrides` gives a zero-boilerplate test seam, but because overrides are global on the app object, a test that forgets to clear them leaks a fake into the next test.","Yield dependencies tie cleanup to the request, which is the right scope for sessions and spans, but their teardown timing relative to the response has shifted across versions, so they are the wrong tool for anything a background task needs."],"pitfalls":["Creating the expensive object inside the dependency (`httpx.AsyncClient()` or a model load per request); symptom: connection pools never warm up, TLS handshakes on every call, memory climbing with concurrency.","Doing blocking work in an `async def` dependency, such as sync JWT verification with a network JWKS fetch; symptom: the whole process stalls during login spikes, not just the authenticating requests.","Using a yield dependency's DB session inside a `BackgroundTasks` function; symptom: intermittent \"session is closed\" errors whose frequency depends on timing and FastAPI version.","Forgetting `dependencies=[Depends(auth)]` on a new router; symptom: one unauthenticated route found in a pentest, because auth was attached per route instead of per router.","Leaving `app.dependency_overrides` set after a test; symptom: a later test passes against the fake model locally and fails when CI runs the files in a different order."],"when":"Use `Depends` for anything a handler needs that is not part of the request body: authentication and\nauthorisation, tenant resolution, sessions and transactions, access to shared clients and models,\nper-request budgets, pagination parameters, feature flags. Put enforcement-only dependencies at router\nlevel. Keep dependencies small and composable, and have them fetch rather than build long-lived\nresources. In tests, override at the boundary where the real world starts: the LLM client, the vector\nstore, the database. Count dependency calls per request when latency is unexplained. In interviews,\nthe questions are \"how would you test this endpoint without calling the model?\" and \"where does the\nDB session get closed if the handler raises?\"; `dependency_overrides` and `yield` with `try/finally`\nare the answers.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes auth, session and client dependencies, uses `Annotated` types for them, and overrides the model client in tests so the suite never spends tokens.","architect":"Defines the dependency boundaries every service shares — principal, tenant, budget, tracing — so teams compose them rather than reinvent them.","fde":"Swaps a customer-specific auth or data-source dependency into an existing service without forking handlers, using overrides to demo against sandbox systems."},"check":{"q":"A service has `get_db` (yield: opens a session, commits on success, rolls back on exception),\n`get_user` (depends on `get_db`), and a `/generate` endpoint depending on both plus `get_llm`. A\nteammate adds a background task that writes the generation result using the session from `get_db`,\nand tests now hit the real provider. Explain what is wrong and how you would restructure.","a":"The background task runs after the handler returns, while the session's lifetime ends after\n`yield`; depending on FastAPI version the session may already be closed when the task runs, giving\nintermittent errors, and in any case a request-scoped resource must not outlive the request. The task\nshould open its own session, or the write should move into the handler before returning, or the work\nshould go to a durable queue. Tests hitting the provider means `get_llm` is not overridden: register\n`app.dependency_overrides[get_llm]` with a fake in a fixture and clear it on teardown. Note that\n`get_db` is resolved once per request thanks to caching, so `get_user` and the handler share one\nsession and one transaction, which is the intended scope."},"quiz":[{"q":"`get_settings` is a dependency of both `get_llm` and `get_retriever`, and an endpoint depends on both. How many times does `get_settings` run per request by default?","options":["Twice, once per dependent","Once; the result is cached for the rest of that request","Once per worker process, at startup","Zero times until a handler accesses the value"],"answer":1,"explanation":"FastAPI caches a dependency's result within a single request when the same callable appears multiple times; `use_cache=False` disables this for dependencies that must run every time."},{"q":"Where should an `httpx.AsyncClient` for the model provider be created, and what should the dependency do?","options":["Created inside the dependency on every request, so each request has a clean client","Created once in lifespan and stored on `app.state`; the dependency returns that shared instance","Created as a module-level global at import time","Created in a `BaseHTTPMiddleware` and attached to the request"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The client owns a connection pool that must persist across requests; the dependency's job is to provide typed, overridable access to the shared object, not to build it."},{"q":"How do tests replace the real LLM client dependency without modifying production code?","options":["Monkeypatch `httpx.AsyncClient.post` globally","Set an environment variable that the dependency checks","Register a fake in `app.dependency_overrides[get_llm]` and clear it afterwards","Subclass `FastAPI` and override `__call__`"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Overrides are keyed by the dependency callable and apply everywhere it is used, including nested dependencies, which makes them the designated seam for test doubles."}]},{"key":"fastapi-for-ai/request-scoped-vs-application-scoped-resources","sources":["Official docs: FastAPI","Official docs: HTTPX"],"why":"Two kinds of mistake are common in AI services, and they are mirror images. The first is building\nexpensive, shareable things per request: `httpx.AsyncClient()` inside the handler,\n`from_pretrained(...)` inside a dependency, a new engine per call. A fresh client means a new TCP and\nTLS handshake for every provider call (tens to hundreds of milliseconds, as an illustrative order of\nmagnitude), no connection reuse, and file descriptors that pile up under load; a fresh model load\nmeans seconds of latency and a copy of the weights per concurrent request until memory runs out. The\nsecond mistake is sharing things that carry per-request state: a module-level `current_user`, a single\nDB session reused across requests, a global list used as a scratch buffer. Under concurrency, two\nrequests interleave on the event loop and one reads the other's user, commits the other's half-finished\ntransaction, or returns the other's documents. These bugs are intermittent, appear only under load,\nand look like data leaks because they are.\n\nThe naive rule \"make everything global for speed\" produces the second class of bug; \"make everything\nfresh for safety\" produces the first. The decision has to be made per object, by asking two\nquestions: is it expensive or stateful across requests (a pool, a cache, loaded weights), and is it\nsafe to use from many concurrent requests at once? Expensive and concurrency-safe → application scope.\nCarries per-request identity or a transaction, or unsafe for concurrent use → request scope. FastAPI\ngives one mechanism for each: lifespan plus `app.state` for the former, `Depends` with `yield` for the\nlatter, and a worker process as the boundary of \"application\".","how":"### Application scope: once per worker\nThe lifespan context manager runs before the worker accepts traffic. Objects created there — the\nprovider client, the engine, the loaded model, the tokenizer, parsed settings — are attached to\n`app.state` (or yielded as lifespan state and read from `request.state`). They live until shutdown,\nwhen the lifespan's exit code closes them. \"Application\" means the worker process: with four Uvicorn\nworkers there are four clients, four engines and four copies of the model, each with its own memory.\nNothing in this scope is shared across processes; cross-process state (rate counters, caches, session\nstores) belongs in Redis or the database.\n\nObjects in this scope are used from many requests at once, so they must be safe for concurrent use.\n`httpx.AsyncClient`, SQLAlchemy's engine and async pool, and provider SDK clients are designed for it.\nA loaded PyTorch model in eval mode can serve concurrent forward passes, but the GPU serialises the\nwork, so a concurrency limit belongs next to it. A raw SQLAlchemy `Session` is not safe to share and\ndoes not belong here.\n\n### Request scope: once per request, torn down with it\nA dependency with `yield` builds the object, hands it to the handler and cleans up afterwards. This is\nthe home for a DB session and its transaction (commit on success, roll back on exception), the\nauthenticated principal, the tenant's resolved configuration, a per-request token budget, the trace\nspan, the request ID. FastAPI's per-request dependency cache guarantees that one request sees one\ninstance even when several dependencies ask for it, which is how a handler and its auth dependency\nshare a single transaction.\n\n### The hand-off between scopes\nRequest-scoped objects are typically *derived from* application-scoped ones: the session comes from the\nengine's pool, the per-request \"view\" of the client is the shared client plus per-request headers, the\nretrieval handle is the shared index plus the tenant's namespace. The dependency borrows from\n`app.state` and returns something cheap. Resources flow one way, application → request, never back; a\nrequest must not stash its session or its user on `app.state`.\n\n### Why the process is the boundary\nPython's event loop interleaves requests within one process, so application scope means \"shared by\nconcurrent requests\" and must be concurrency-safe. Across processes nothing is shared, so application\nscope also means \"duplicated per worker\" and must be affordable N times. Both facts come from the same\narchitecture: Uvicorn workers are independent processes with independent loops.\n\n### Context variables\nFor data that must follow a request through code that cannot take parameters (logging formatters, deep\nlibrary calls), `contextvars` give request-scoped storage without globals: set the request ID in a\nmiddleware or dependency, read it anywhere in that task. Each request's task sees its own value, which\nis exactly the isolation a module global cannot give.","example":"Lifespan creates the application-scoped objects; dependencies derive request-scoped ones; the handler\nmixes them without ever creating a client or sharing a session. `Document` and `Summary` are ORM\nclasses defined elsewhere.\n\n```python\nfrom contextlib import asynccontextmanager\nfrom typing import Annotated\nimport httpx\nfrom fastapi import Depends, FastAPI, Header, Request\nfrom sqlalchemy.ext.asyncio import AsyncSession, async_sessionmaker, create_async_engine\n\n@asynccontextmanager\nasync def lifespan(app: FastAPI):\n    app.state.http = httpx.AsyncClient(base_url=\"https://api.example.com\", timeout=30)  # pool shared\n    app.state.engine = create_async_engine(\"postgresql+asyncpg://user:pw@db/app\", pool_size=10)\n    app.state.sessions = async_sessionmaker(app.state.engine, expire_on_commit=False)\n    yield\n    await app.state.http.aclose()            # application scope ends with the worker\n    await app.state.engine.dispose()\n\napp = FastAPI(lifespan=lifespan)\n\nasync def db(request: Request):              # request scope: one session, one transaction\n    async with request.app.state.sessions() as session:\n        async with session.begin():           # commits on success, rolls back on exception\n            yield session\n\ndef principal(x_api_key: Annotated[str, Header()]) -> str:\n    return x_api_key.split(\"-\")[0]           # request scope: derived from this request's headers\n\n@app.post(\"/summarise\")\nasync def summarise(doc_id: int, request: Request,\n                    session: Annotated[AsyncSession, Depends(db)],\n                    who: Annotated[str, Depends(principal)]):\n    http = request.app.state.http             # borrow the shared client; never httpx.AsyncClient() here\n    doc = await session.get(Document, doc_id)\n    r = await http.post(\"/v1/complete\", json={\"text\": doc.text}, headers={\"x-tenant\": who})\n    session.add(Summary(doc_id=doc_id, text=r.json()[\"text\"], by=who))\n    return {\"ok\": True}\n```","tradeoffs":["Application scope amortises expensive setup across all requests, but everything in it is duplicated per worker; past a couple of workers the memory of a loaded model forces a separate model server.","Request scope guarantees isolation and clean teardown at the cost of per-request construction; it flips to a problem only when the object is expensive, which is the signal to move construction to lifespan and keep only a cheap handle per request.","Sharing a concurrency-safe client gives connection reuse and pool limits in one place, but one bad setting (a tiny pool, a global timeout) now affects every endpoint at once.","`contextvars` remove the need to thread request context through signatures, but values set inside a thread-pool call or a separate task do not flow back, so the mechanism is only as predictable as the task structure around it."],"pitfalls":["Creating `httpx.AsyncClient()` or a provider SDK client per request; symptom: elevated provider latency from handshakes, \"too many open files\" under load, and no benefit from keep-alive.","Loading a model in a dependency or on first request; symptom: a multi-second first call per worker, memory growing with concurrency, and OOM kills once several workers run.","Storing the current user or request-specific data on `app.state` or a module global; symptom: intermittent wrong-user responses that reproduce only under concurrent load.","Sharing one DB session across requests; symptom: \"session already in a transaction\" errors and commits that contain other requests' changes.","Expecting an in-process cache or counter on `app.state` to be global; symptom: rate limits enforced per worker, so a limit of 100 becomes 400 with four workers."],"when":"Decide scope for each object by writing it down: cost to create, whether it is safe for concurrent\nuse, whether it carries per-request identity. Clients, engines, models, indexes and settings go to\nlifespan; sessions, principals, budgets, spans and IDs go to dependencies; anything that must be shared\nacross workers or survive a restart goes to an external store. Measure connection reuse (new\nconnections per second on the provider client), time-to-first-request per worker, and memory per\nworker; each points at a scoping mistake when it is wrong. This decision comes up in interviews as\n\"where would you create the OpenAI client and why not in the endpoint?\", and in code review every time\na `Client()` call appears inside a function that runs per request.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Builds clients, engines and models in lifespan, derives sessions and principals in yield dependencies, and never instantiates a client inside a handler.","architect":"Sets the worker-versus-model-server boundary from per-worker memory, and decides which state must leave the process for Redis or the database.","fde":"Finds the per-request client or the shared session in a customer's codebase when they report handshake latency or cross-tenant data appearing in responses."},"check":{"q":"A multi-tenant RAG service shows two symptoms after scaling to four workers: provider latency\nrose noticeably per call, and a customer once saw another tenant's document titles. The code creates\nan `AsyncClient` in each handler and keeps the current tenant in a module-level variable set by a\nmiddleware. Explain each symptom and the fix.","a":"The latency is the handshake cost of a new client per request: no pooled connections are\nreused, so every provider call pays TCP and TLS setup; move the client to lifespan on `app.state` and\nborrow it from a dependency. The leak is a request-scoped value held in application scope: the\nmiddleware sets a module global that every concurrent request in the worker reads, so when request B's\nmiddleware runs while request A awaits the provider, A's retrieval uses B's tenant. Replace the global\nwith a dependency that resolves the tenant from the request (or a `contextvar` set per request) and\npass it into the retrieval call. Both are the same mistake in opposite directions: an expensive\nshareable thing was made per request, and a per-request thing was made shared."},"quiz":[{"q":"Which object belongs in request scope rather than application scope?","options":["The tokenizer used for counting prompt tokens","The `httpx.AsyncClient` used to call the model provider","The database session and its transaction for the current call","The parsed settings object with provider keys and limits"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A session carries a transaction and is not safe for concurrent use, so each request needs its own, derived from the application-scoped engine and torn down after the handler finishes."},{"q":"With `uvicorn --workers 4`, what does \"application-scoped\" imply for a loaded model?","options":["One copy shared by all workers through shared memory","Four independent copies, one per worker process, each loaded in that worker's lifespan","One copy in the master and lazy copies on first request","The model is reloaded whenever a worker restarts a request"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Workers are separate processes with separate event loops and app instances; application scope means once per process, which is why large models usually move to a dedicated server."},{"q":"Why is keeping the current user in a module-level variable a bug in an async FastAPI service?","options":["Module globals are read-only in async code","FastAPI resets module globals between requests","Concurrent requests interleave on the loop and overwrite each other's value","Module globals are not visible from dependencies"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Request-scoped identity stored in application scope is shared by every in-flight request in the process; a dependency or a context variable keeps it isolated per request."}]},{"key":"fastapi-for-ai/streamingresponse","sources":["Official docs: FastAPI (Custom Response, StreamingResponse)","Official docs: Starlette (Responses)","Official docs: ASGI specification (HTTP connection scope and messages)"],"why":"The default FastAPI path is: the handler returns a value, FastAPI serialises it, Starlette sets\n`Content-Length` and writes the body in one go. That is right for a 2 KB JSON answer and wrong for\nanything produced incrementally. A 600-token answer decoded at roughly 40 tokens per second (an\nillustrative figure; it varies by model and load) exists in full only after about 15 seconds, and\nuntil then the client sees nothing. Three things go wrong at once. The user experience collapses,\nbecause people judge responsiveness by the first visible change. Infrastructure timeouts trip:\nreverse proxies and load balancers commonly ship with request or idle timeouts in the tens of seconds,\nso long answers die with a 502 or 504 even though the model was still working. And memory scales with\nconcurrency, because every in-flight request keeps its whole output alive until the end.\n\nThe naive fixes fail. Raising every timeout keeps the connection open but does nothing for first-token\nlatency and still ties up a connection per request. Polling a job endpoint adds a round trip per poll\nand a latency granularity equal to the poll interval. WebSockets solve a different problem, two-way\ntraffic, and bring a stateful connection with them. And you cannot simply return a generator from a\nnormal endpoint: FastAPI would try to serialise it as JSON. You need an explicit opt-out from the\nbuffered response pipeline, and that opt-out is `StreamingResponse`.","how":"### What you hand over\n`StreamingResponse(content, status_code=200, headers=None, media_type=None, background=None)` takes\nany iterable or async iterable that yields `bytes` or `str` (strings are encoded with the response's\ncharset, UTF-8 by default). The class is Starlette's; FastAPI re-exports it from `fastapi.responses`.\nBecause you construct the response yourself, `response_model` validation and FastAPI's JSON encoding\nare bypassed: what you yield is what goes on the wire.\n\n### What happens at the ASGI layer\nA normal response sends one `http.response.start` message (status, headers) followed by a single\n`http.response.body` with `more_body: False`. A streaming response sends the start message, then one\n`http.response.body` with `more_body: True` per chunk, then an empty body with `more_body: False` to\nclose. No `Content-Length` is known up front, so over HTTP/1.1 the server (uvicorn) uses\n`Transfer-Encoding: chunked`; behind a proxy that terminates HTTP/2 the chunks simply become DATA\nframes. This is why you must not set `Content-Length` yourself unless you really know it.\n\n### Sync versus async iterators\nIf `content` is a plain generator, Starlette drives it with `iterate_in_threadpool`: each `next()` is\nrun on a worker thread so it cannot block the event loop. That is safe but costs a thread hop per\nchunk and occupies a slot in the shared thread pool (anyio's default limiter is 40 threads) for the\nlife of the stream. An `async def` generator is awaited directly on the loop, which is what you want\nwhen the source is an async SDK stream or an `httpx` streaming response. Mixing the two, for example\ncalling a blocking SDK inside an async generator, blocks every other request for the duration of\neach call.\n\n### Flow control\nEach `await send(chunk)` returns only when the server has accepted the chunk. Uvicorn pauses writers\nwhen the transport's buffer passes its high-water mark, so a client that reads slowly eventually\nstalls the generator. That is natural backpressure, but it engages only after the OS and server\nbuffers fill, so a stalled client can still hold tens of kilobytes of generated text and, more\nimportantly, an upstream model stream that keeps billing tokens.\n\n### Disconnects\nWhen the client goes away the server delivers an `http.disconnect` event. Recent Starlette versions\nrun the generator inside a task group next to a listener for that event and cancel the generator\nwhen it fires, so an `async for` over the model stream receives `CancelledError` and can propagate\nthe cancellation upstream. The exact behaviour has changed across Starlette releases, so the robust\npattern is to also check `await request.is_disconnected()` inside the loop and to put cleanup in\n`try/finally`. The point is economic as much as technical: tokens generated after the user closed the\ntab are pure cost.\n\n### Headers are frozen at the first chunk\nStatus and headers go out before the first body chunk. An exception raised by the generator after\nthat cannot become a 500; the client sees a truncated body and, with chunked encoding, a missing\nterminating chunk that most clients report as a protocol error. So do everything that can fail,\nauthentication, quota checks, input validation, ideally even the first call to the model, *before*\nreturning the `StreamingResponse`, and signal failures during the stream in-band with a typed chunk.\n\n### Framing\nTCP does not preserve your `yield` boundaries: a client may receive two chunks glued together or one\nchunk split, even in the middle of a multi-byte character. Raw text is therefore only acceptable when\nthe client treats the body as a single growing string. For anything structured pick a framing and set\n`media_type` to match: newline-delimited JSON (`application/x-ndjson`), Server-Sent Events\n(`text/event-stream`, covered in its own lesson), or length-prefixed binary.\n\n### Middleware and proxies\nAny component that waits for the whole body destroys the benefit. nginx buffers proxied responses by\ndefault (`proxy_buffering on`); send `X-Accel-Buffering: no` or turn buffering off for the route.\nSome CDNs and API gateways buffer too, and compression middleware can delay small chunks while it\nfills a compression window. Always test with `curl -N` against the real ingress, not just uvicorn on\nlocalhost.","example":"An NDJSON token stream. Validation happens before the response object is created, the loop stops\ngenerating when the client has gone, and a failure after the first byte becomes a typed error line\ninstead of a truncated body.\n\n```python\nimport asyncio, json\nfrom fastapi import FastAPI, HTTPException, Request\nfrom fastapi.responses import StreamingResponse\nfrom pydantic import BaseModel\n\napp = FastAPI()\n\nclass Ask(BaseModel):\n    prompt: str\n\nasync def model_stream(prompt: str):\n    # Stand-in for your SDK's async streaming iterator; both major vendor SDKs expose one.\n    for word in \"Streaming keeps the user reading while the model is still decoding.\".split():\n        await asyncio.sleep(0.05)\n        yield word + \" \"\n\n@app.post(\"/answer\")\nasync def answer(body: Ask, request: Request):\n    if len(body.prompt) > 8000:\n        raise HTTPException(413, \"prompt too long\")      # fail BEFORE any byte of the stream is sent\n\n    async def ndjson():\n        try:\n            async for piece in model_stream(body.prompt):\n                if await request.is_disconnected():\n                    break                                 # stop paying for tokens nobody will read\n                yield json.dumps({\"delta\": piece}) + \"\\n\"\n            yield json.dumps({\"done\": True}) + \"\\n\"\n        except Exception as exc:                          # headers are gone: signal the error in-band\n            yield json.dumps({\"error\": type(exc).__name__}) + \"\\n\"\n\n    return StreamingResponse(ndjson(), media_type=\"application/x-ndjson\",\n                             headers={\"Cache-Control\": \"no-cache\", \"X-Accel-Buffering\": \"no\"})\n\n# Check it really streams through your ingress, not just locally:\n#   curl -N -X POST http://localhost:8000/answer -H 'content-type: application/json' -d '{\"prompt\":\"hi\"}'\n```","tradeoffs":["First-token latency drops to the model's own TTFT, but total server time per request does not shrink and the connection stays open for the whole generation, so capacity must be sized by concurrent streams rather than by requests per second.","You stop buffering whole bodies in memory, but you lose `Content-Length`, so clients cannot show a percentage and HTTP caches will not store the response; for small, cacheable payloads a plain `JSONResponse` is the better choice.","In-band error chunks let the client keep partial output, but every consumer must now parse a protocol instead of checking a status code; when the consumers are third-party integrators expecting REST semantics, offer a non-streaming variant as well.","A sync generator works without rewriting a blocking source, but every chunk costs a thread hop; fine for reading a file in 64 KB blocks, wasteful for thousands of token-sized chunks per second."],"pitfalls":["Raising `HTTPException` inside the generator; the symptom is a client-side \"incomplete chunked encoding\" or truncated body while the server log shows an exception after a 200 was already sent.","A buffering proxy between you and the user; the symptom is a stream that works with uvicorn locally but arrives as one burst at the very end in production, often followed by gateway timeouts on long answers.","Using a request-scoped resource from a `yield` dependency (a DB session) inside the generator; its teardown can run before the generator body executes, and the timing has changed across FastAPI versions, so the symptom is \"session is closed\" errors that appear only after an upgrade.","Yielding raw text and assuming one `yield` equals one client read; the symptom is tokens glued together or a split multi-byte character that decodes as garbage on the client.","Awaiting nothing while calling a blocking SDK inside an `async def` generator; the symptom is that every other request, including health checks, stalls while one stream is being produced."],"when":"Reach for `StreamingResponse` whenever output is produced incrementally and the consumer benefits from\nseeing it early: LLM token streams, long agent runs that emit progress, large exports generated row by\nrow, proxying an upstream streaming API. Do not use it for small responses, for anything a CDN should\ncache, or as a substitute for a job queue when the work must survive a disconnect. Measure time to\nfirst byte at the client, bytes in flight per connection, and the fraction of streams cancelled by\ndisconnects. In interviews the expected depth is the ASGI message sequence, why headers are frozen,\nhow disconnects and backpressure reach the generator, and what breaks at the proxy layer.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes async generators that wrap the model SDK's stream, frames chunks as NDJSON or SSE, validates before the first byte and checks for disconnects inside the loop.","architect":"Decides which routes stream, sets ingress and load-balancer buffering and timeout policy for them, and sizes capacity by concurrent open streams instead of request rate.","fde":"Diagnoses the customer's \"it works in staging but not behind our gateway\" by walking the path from uvicorn through nginx, API gateway and CDN looking for the buffer."},"check":{"q":"A streaming chat endpoint works locally, but in production tokens arrive in a single burst\nwhen the answer finishes, and answers longer than about a minute fail with a 504. Diagnose it.","a":"Both symptoms point at an intermediary that buffers the response. The burst means something is\ncollecting chunks until the body completes: nginx `proxy_buffering`, an API gateway or CDN, or a\ncompression layer. Because no bytes reach the load balancer while it buffers, the balancer's idle\ntimeout fires on long answers and returns 504. Confirm with `curl -N` against each hop in turn, then\ndisable buffering for the route (`X-Accel-Buffering: no` or `proxy_buffering off`), exclude the route\nfrom compression, and raise the idle timeout only as a last resort. Also check that the endpoint is\nnot accidentally setting `Content-Length`, and add periodic keepalive chunks for silent phases."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why can a StreamingResponse not change its status code to 500 when the generator fails halfway?","options":["Starlette only allows status changes for JSONResponse","Status and headers are sent in the `http.response.start` message before the first chunk, so they cannot be revised","Chunked transfer encoding does not support status codes","The event loop has already moved on to the next request"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The ASGI protocol emits the start message once, before any body bytes. After that, only body chunks can follow, which is why mid-stream failures must be signalled in-band as a typed chunk."},{"q":"What is the main reason to prefer an async generator over a sync generator as the content?","options":["Sync generators cannot yield bytes","Async generators automatically set Content-Length","Each item from a sync generator is pulled on a thread-pool thread, costing a hop per chunk and a pool slot for the stream's lifetime","Sync generators bypass disconnect detection entirely"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Starlette wraps sync iterators with `iterate_in_threadpool` to keep the loop unblocked. That is correct but expensive for thousands of small chunks and it consumes a limited thread pool."},{"q":"A client disconnects in the middle of a token stream. What should the endpoint be designed to do?","options":["Nothing; the server automatically returns the unused tokens to the model provider","Retry the request so the client receives the full answer on reconnect","Detect the disconnect, stop iterating, and cancel the upstream model call so no further tokens are generated or billed","Switch the response to a BackgroundTask so generation completes anyway"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Tokens generated after the client left are pure cost. Starlette cancels the generator on disconnect in recent versions, and checking `request.is_disconnected()` with cleanup in `finally` makes that behaviour explicit and version-independent."}]},{"key":"fastapi-for-ai/server-sent-events","sources":["Official docs: WHATWG HTML Living Standard (server-sent events)","Official docs: MDN (Using server-sent events)","Official docs: sse-starlette"],"why":"`StreamingResponse` gets bytes out early, but bytes are not messages. A chat client needs to tell a\ntoken delta from a tool-call announcement from a usage report from an error, and TCP may split or\nmerge whatever the server yielded. Every team that starts with raw chunks ends up inventing a framing,\nand every client then reimplements the parser slightly differently.\n\nThe obvious alternative is WebSockets, and for one-way traffic it is the wrong tool: an upgrade\nhandshake, a stateful connection pinned to one process, no automatic reconnection, no standard way to\nresume, and infrastructure that must understand upgrades. Token streaming is server-to-client; the\nonly client input is the request that started it.\n\nSSE sits in the gap. It was standardised as part of the HTML specification, so every browser ships a\nparser (`EventSource`), the stream is a normal HTTP response that proxies, load balancers and\nauthentication middleware already understand, HTTP/2 multiplexes many streams over one connection, and\nthe format has two features a token stream needs: a typed `event:` field so one stream can carry tokens\nand control messages, and an `id:` field so a client that drops mid-answer can ask for the rest instead\nof paying for the generation again. Those two fields are the whole reason it beats \"newline-delimited\nJSON\" for user-facing LLM streams.","how":"### The wire format\n```\nevent: token\ndata: {\"delta\": \"Hel\"}\nid: 1\n\nevent: token\ndata: {\"delta\": \"lo\"}\nid: 2\n\n: keepalive\n\nevent: done\ndata: {\"output_tokens\": 2}\n\n```\nEach line is `field: value`. A blank line dispatches the event. Rules that matter in practice: `data:`\nis the payload and several `data:` lines in one event are joined with newlines; `event:` names the type\n(default `message`); `id:` sets the client's last-event id; `retry:` tells the client how many\nmilliseconds to wait before reconnecting; a line starting with `:` is a comment that clients discard,\nwhich makes it a free keepalive. The body is UTF-8 text, so binary payloads must be base64-encoded.\nThe response carries `Content-Type: text/event-stream` and should carry `Cache-Control: no-cache`;\nbehind nginx add `X-Accel-Buffering: no`.\n\n### The client side\n`EventSource` is built into browsers: it opens a GET, parses events, fires listeners by `event:` name,\nreconnects automatically when the connection drops and sends the `Last-Event-ID` header on reconnect.\nIts limits decide how chat UIs are built: it can only GET, cannot set an `Authorization` header or a\nbody, and sends credentials only via cookies. A chat prompt is a POST with a JSON body and a bearer\ntoken, so production frontends use `fetch()` and parse the stream themselves (a `ReadableStream`, a\n`TextDecoder` and a line splitter, or a small library), which means they also own reconnection logic.\nServer-side consumers use `httpx` with `stream=True` and `aiter_lines()`; vendor SDKs parse the\nvendor's own SSE for you.\n\n### The server side in FastAPI\nThe minimum is a `StreamingResponse` whose generator yields correctly formatted strings with\n`media_type=\"text/event-stream\"`. The `sse-starlette` package adds an `EventSourceResponse` that\nformats dict events, sends periodic ping comments, and handles client disconnects, which removes the\nmost common mistakes. Either way the endpoint is an ordinary route: dependencies, auth and validation\nrun before the first byte, which is exactly when errors can still be reported with a status code.\n\n### Keepalives and intermediaries\nLoad balancers and proxies drop connections that carry no bytes for a configurable idle period, often\non the order of a minute by default (vendor-dependent). An agent that spends ninety seconds inside a\ntool call before producing the next token looks idle. Sending a comment line every 10 to 20 seconds\nkeeps the timers reset at no cost to the client. The same intermediaries must also not buffer; see\nthe StreamingResponse lesson.\n\n### HTTP/1.1 connection limits\nBrowsers limit concurrent HTTP/1.1 connections per origin to a small number, around six, and every open\n`EventSource` holds one. Several tabs with live streams can starve the rest of the app. HTTP/2 lifts\nthe limit by multiplexing, so terminate HTTP/2 at the edge; uvicorn speaking HTTP/1.1 behind that\nproxy is fine.\n\n### Resumability\n`Last-Event-ID` is only useful if the server can replay. The pattern: give each run an id, assign\nmonotonic event ids, append every event to a short-lived buffer (a Redis list with a TTL keyed by run\nid) as it is produced, and on a request carrying `Last-Event-ID` replay the buffered events after that\nid before continuing live. For agent runs that cost real money per attempt this converts a flaky\nmobile connection from \"re-run the whole thing\" into \"catch up\".\n\n### Event vocabularies\nOpenAI's API streams JSON chunks in `data:` lines and ends with a literal `data: [DONE]`; Anthropic's\nMessages API streams typed events such as `message_start`, `content_block_delta` and `message_stop`.\nYour own API should define its own small vocabulary (`token`, `tool_call`, `usage`, `done`, `error`)\nand translate, so that swapping the model provider never changes the frontend.","example":"A FastAPI SSE endpoint built directly on `StreamingResponse`. The model stream runs in its own task\nfeeding a queue, so the consumer can wait with a timeout and emit a keepalive comment during silent\nphases without cancelling the producer. Each event carries an id so a client can resume.\n\n```python\nimport asyncio, json\nfrom fastapi import FastAPI\nfrom fastapi.responses import StreamingResponse\nfrom pydantic import BaseModel\n\napp = FastAPI()\n\nclass Ask(BaseModel):\n    prompt: str\n\ndef sse(event: str, data: dict, id: int) -> str:\n    return f\"event: {event}\\nid: {id}\\ndata: {json.dumps(data)}\\n\\n\"   # blank line dispatches\n\nasync def model_stream(prompt: str):                  # stand-in for your SDK's async stream\n    for w in \"Server-sent events frame the tokens.\".split():\n        await asyncio.sleep(0.3); yield w + \" \"\n\n@app.post(\"/chat\")\nasync def chat(body: Ask):\n    q: asyncio.Queue = asyncio.Queue()\n\n    async def produce():\n        try:\n            async for piece in model_stream(body.prompt):\n                await q.put((\"token\", {\"delta\": piece}))\n            await q.put((\"done\", {}))\n        except Exception as exc:\n            await q.put((\"error\", {\"message\": str(exc)}))\n\n    async def events():\n        task, n = asyncio.create_task(produce()), 0\n        try:\n            while True:\n                try:\n                    event, data = await asyncio.wait_for(q.get(), timeout=15)\n                except asyncio.TimeoutError:\n                    yield \": ping\\n\\n\"; continue       # keepalive comment; clients ignore it\n                n += 1\n                yield sse(event, data, n)\n                if event in (\"done\", \"error\"):\n                    break\n        finally:\n            task.cancel()                              # client gone or stream over: stop generating\n    return StreamingResponse(events(), media_type=\"text/event-stream\",\n                             headers={\"Cache-Control\": \"no-cache\", \"X-Accel-Buffering\": \"no\"})\n```","tradeoffs":["Plain HTTP means SSE passes through proxies, auth middleware and HTTP/2 multiplexing untouched, but it is one-directional: an \"interrupt generation\" button needs a second request plus shared state, and once the client must talk continuously (voice, collaborative editing) WebSockets win.","Text-only framing keeps parsers trivial, but binary payloads such as audio must be base64-encoded, adding about a third to their size; at that point a binary WebSocket frame is cheaper.","Automatic reconnection with `Last-Event-ID` gives resumability for almost no client code, but only if the server buffers events per run; without the buffer a reconnect silently restarts and double-bills the generation.","`EventSource` is zero code but GET-only and header-less; parsing with `fetch()` gives you POST bodies and bearer tokens at the cost of owning reconnection and the parser yourself."],"pitfalls":["Forgetting the terminating blank line or using a single newline between fields; the symptom is a client that receives nothing until the stream closes, then everything as one malformed event.","Putting raw text with embedded newlines into one `data:` line; the symptom is events split at the wrong place, which JSON-encoding the payload (newlines become `\\n`) avoids entirely.","No keepalive during long tool calls; the symptom is streams that die at a fixed wall-clock interval, often exactly the load balancer's idle timeout, only when the agent is \"thinking\".","A buffering proxy or compression middleware on the route; the symptom is all events arriving in a burst at the end, with the server logs showing they were produced on time.","Leaking the vendor's event schema straight to the browser; the symptom is a model-provider switch that breaks the frontend parser.","Replaying a run from `Last-Event-ID` without re-checking that the caller owns that run; the symptom is one user resuming another user's stream by guessing an id."],"when":"Use SSE for anything the server pushes and the client only consumes: token streams, agent progress\nand tool-call events, job status updates, live evaluation dashboards. Prefer it over WebSockets by\ndefault because it keeps the stateless HTTP model, and move to WebSockets only when the client must\nsend during the stream or needs binary frames. Measure client-observed time to first event, event\ncounts per run, reconnect rate and the share of streams lost to idle timeouts. In interviews, expect to\nwrite the frame format from memory, explain why chat UIs cannot use `EventSource` for POST, and\ndescribe how `id:` plus a per-run buffer makes a stream resumable.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Implements the event vocabulary, the formatter and the keepalive, and makes sure every failure after the first byte is emitted as an `error` event rather than a dropped connection.","architect":"Chooses SSE versus WebSockets per route, mandates HTTP/2 at the edge, and defines the resumability contract (run ids, event ids, buffer TTL) that clients can rely on.","fde":"Traces a customer's dying streams to an idle timeout on their corporate proxy and fixes it with keepalive comments instead of asking their network team for an exception."},"check":{"q":"A mobile client loses connectivity thirty seconds into a two-minute agent run. Design the server\nso the user does not pay for a second run, and explain what the client must do.","a":"Give each run an id when it starts and assign monotonically increasing `id:` values to every\nevent. As events are produced, append them to a per-run buffer with a TTL, for example a Redis list,\nin the same step that sends them. On reconnect the client sends `Last-Event-ID` (automatically with\n`EventSource`, manually with a `fetch`-based client) to a resume endpoint that first checks the caller\nowns the run, replays buffered events after that id, then attaches to the live stream. Generation\nitself must keep running independently of the HTTP connection, so it belongs in a worker task or job\nrather than inside the request's generator, and the response generator becomes a reader of the buffer."},"quiz":[{"q":"What does a line beginning with a colon mean in an SSE stream?","options":["It begins a multi-line data payload","It is a comment the client ignores, which makes it a free keepalive to reset idle timers","It sets the reconnection delay in milliseconds","It terminates the current event"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Comment lines are discarded by every conforming parser, so a periodic `: ping` keeps proxies and load balancers from closing a connection that is silent while the model or a tool is working."},{"q":"Why do chat frontends usually parse SSE from a fetch() call instead of using EventSource?","options":["EventSource cannot parse the `event:` field","fetch() reconnects automatically while EventSource does not","EventSource only issues GET requests without custom headers or a body, but a chat turn is a POST with JSON and a bearer token","Browsers limit EventSource to one connection per page"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The built-in API has no way to send a request body or an Authorization header. Fetch-based parsing gives POST and headers back, at the cost of implementing reconnection and the line parser yourself."},{"q":"What must the server do for Last-Event-ID based resumption to work?","options":["Set `retry:` on every event","Use WebSockets, because SSE cannot resume","Assign event ids and buffer each run's events so it can replay those after the id the client last saw","Send `Content-Length` so the client knows how much is missing"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Reconnection only carries the last id the client saw; replaying from that point requires the server to have kept the events. Without a per-run buffer the reconnect simply starts a new generation."}]},{"key":"fastapi-for-ai/websockets","sources":["Fette & Melnikov, 2011 — RFC 6455, The WebSocket Protocol","Official docs: FastAPI (WebSockets)","Official docs: ASGI specification (WebSocket connection scope)","Official docs: Uvicorn (settings)"],"why":"HTTP is request then response. Once the client has sent its body it cannot say anything else on that\nexchange, and the server cannot push anything the client did not ask for. Two AI interactions break\nthat model. The first is interruption: a user watching tokens arrive over SSE presses stop, and that\nstop must reach the task producing the tokens. Over HTTP it becomes a second request, which the load\nbalancer may route to a different worker process, so now you need shared state (a Redis pub/sub\nchannel, a cancel flag in a store) just to deliver one bit to one coroutine. The second is continuous\ntwo-way media: a voice agent receives audio frames every few tens of milliseconds while sending\nsynthesized audio back. A request per frame is absurd in overhead and ordering; long-polling hacks add\nlatency and complexity without removing either problem.\n\nThe WebSocket protocol (RFC 6455) was designed for exactly this: a single TCP connection, established\nthrough an HTTP handshake so it passes existing infrastructure, after which both sides send messages\nwith a two-to-fourteen-byte frame header and no further HTTP ceremony. Both AI needs map onto it\ndirectly: the stop message arrives on the same socket the tokens are leaving on, so it lands in the\nsame process with no shared state, and audio travels as binary frames without base64. What you accept\nin exchange is that the connection, not the request, is now the unit of state.","how":"### The handshake\nThe client sends a GET with `Upgrade: websocket`, `Connection: Upgrade` and a random\n`Sec-WebSocket-Key`. The server answers `101 Switching Protocols` with `Sec-WebSocket-Accept`, a hash\nof the key and a fixed GUID that proves it understood the upgrade. From then on the bytes are frames,\nnot HTTP. Two consequences follow from the handshake being a plain GET. Browsers cannot attach an\n`Authorization` header to it, because the JavaScript `WebSocket` API has no header option, so auth\narrives via a cookie, a short-lived token in the query string (which lands in access logs), or a first\nmessage after connect that the server must receive before doing any work. And CORS does not protect\nit: if browsers are clients and cookies carry auth, check the `Origin` header yourself, otherwise any\nsite can open a socket to your API with the user's cookies.\n\n### Frames, messages and closes\nMessages are text (must be valid UTF-8) or binary. Control frames carry ping/pong, which servers use to\ndetect dead peers, and close, which carries a code: 1000 normal, 1001 going away (server shutting\ndown), 1008 policy violation (a common choice for auth failures), 1011 internal error. Uvicorn exposes\nthe knobs: `--ws-max-size` caps a message (default 16 MiB), and `--ws-ping-interval` /\n`--ws-ping-timeout` (20 seconds each by default) decide how quickly a silent, dead client is dropped.\nUvicorn needs the `websockets` or `wsproto` package for WebSocket support, which `uvicorn[standard]`\ninstalls.\n\n### ASGI and FastAPI\nA WebSocket arrives as a scope of type `websocket`. The app receives `websocket.connect`, answers with\n`websocket.accept` (or `websocket.close` to refuse, which the server turns into an HTTP 403 before\nupgrade), then exchanges `websocket.receive` and `websocket.send` until `websocket.disconnect`.\nFastAPI wraps this: `@app.websocket(\"/ws\")`, a `WebSocket` parameter, `await websocket.accept()`,\n`receive_text/json/bytes` and `send_text/json/bytes`, and a `WebSocketDisconnect` exception when the\npeer closes. Dependencies, path and query parameters, cookies and headers all work in WebSocket routes.\n`HTTPException` does not fit a connection that is no longer HTTP; raise `WebSocketException(code=...)`\nfrom a dependency instead, and FastAPI closes with that code.\n\n### Concurrency inside the handler\nA handler is one coroutine. If it awaits `receive_json()` it is not producing tokens, and if it is\niterating the model stream it is not listening, so a sequential loop can never implement interruption.\nThe pattern is two concurrent activities: a reader loop that handles incoming messages, and a\ngeneration task started with `asyncio.create_task` that the reader can signal (an `asyncio.Event`) or\ncancel. Funnel all outgoing sends through one place, for example the generation task or a single\nsender task fed by a queue, so two tasks never interleave frames.\n\n### Scaling and state\nThe socket lives in one worker process. Messages from the client reach that process for free, which is\nthe point, but anything originating elsewhere (a job finishing on a worker machine, a message from\nanother user) must be fanned out to the process holding the socket, usually through Redis pub/sub.\nLoad balancers must allow upgrades and keep long idle connections alive; many default to about a\nminute of idle time, so application-level pings matter. Rolling deploys must close sockets deliberately\n(code 1001) and clients must reconnect and resume, which means you design a session id and sequence\nnumbers yourself: there is no `Last-Event-ID` equivalent.\n\n### Backpressure\n`await websocket.send_text()` blocks when the peer reads slowly and the buffers fill, which throttles\nthe generation loop, exactly what you want. Sending with fire-and-forget tasks removes that coupling\nand lets memory grow without bound. On the receive side the libraries keep a bounded queue and apply\nTCP backpressure when it is full.","example":"A chat socket where the server streams tokens from a generation task while the reader loop stays free\nto receive a `stop` message, which interrupts the current answer. Auth is a query token because the\nbrowser cannot send headers on the upgrade.\n\n```python\nimport asyncio\nfrom fastapi import FastAPI, WebSocket, WebSocketDisconnect, WebSocketException, status\n\napp = FastAPI()\n\nasync def model_stream(prompt: str):                 # stand-in for your SDK's async stream\n    for w in \"A WebSocket lets the user interrupt this sentence midway.\".split():\n        await asyncio.sleep(0.4); yield w + \" \"\n\nasync def generate(ws: WebSocket, prompt: str, stop: asyncio.Event):\n    async for piece in model_stream(prompt):\n        if stop.is_set():\n            await ws.send_json({\"type\": \"stopped\"}); return\n        await ws.send_json({\"type\": \"token\", \"delta\": piece})\n    await ws.send_json({\"type\": \"done\"})\n\n@app.websocket(\"/ws/chat\")\nasync def chat(ws: WebSocket, token: str):           # ?token=... (short-lived); verify before accept\n    if token != \"demo\":\n        raise WebSocketException(code=status.WS_1008_POLICY_VIOLATION)\n    await ws.accept()\n    stop, task = asyncio.Event(), None\n    try:\n        while True:                                   # reader loop: never blocked by generation\n            msg = await ws.receive_json()\n            if msg[\"type\"] == \"prompt\":\n                if task and not task.done():\n                    stop.set(); await task             # finish the old answer cleanly first\n                stop = asyncio.Event()\n                task = asyncio.create_task(generate(ws, msg[\"text\"], stop))\n            elif msg[\"type\"] == \"stop\":\n                stop.set()\n    except WebSocketDisconnect:\n        pass\n    finally:\n        if task:\n            task.cancel()                             # client gone: stop paying for tokens\n```","tradeoffs":["Full-duplex messaging with tiny per-message overhead, but the connection is stateful and pinned to a process, so cross-process delivery needs pub/sub; when traffic is one-way the statelessness of SSE is worth more than the duplex channel.","Binary frames carry audio and images without base64, but text frames must be valid UTF-8 and the server validates every one; for JSON-only protocols that validation is wasted work compared with SSE.","One long-lived connection avoids a handshake and auth check per message, but thousands of idle sockets each hold memory and a slot in load balancers and worker processes, and shutdowns become a coordination problem instead of waiting for in-flight requests to finish.","No built-in reconnection or resume means you design session ids and sequence numbers, which is more work than SSE's `Last-Event-ID` but also lets you resume with arbitrary semantics."],"pitfalls":["A sequential handler that awaits `receive` and then generates; the symptom is a stop button that only takes effect after the answer has finished.","Putting a long-lived credential in the query string; the symptom is secrets in access logs and proxy logs, which is why upgrade tokens must be short-lived or auth must happen in the first message.","An ingress not configured for upgrades or with a short idle timeout; the symptom is 400 or 502 on connect, or every socket dying after exactly the same number of silent seconds.","Raising `HTTPException` in a WebSocket route or dependency; the symptom is an unhelpful 500 or an abrupt close instead of a meaningful close code.","Sending from several tasks or with fire-and-forget `create_task`; the symptom is interleaved frames, `RuntimeError: Cannot call \"send\" once a close message has been sent`, or memory growth with slow clients.","Not cancelling the generation task on `WebSocketDisconnect`; the symptom is model spend continuing after users close the tab, visible as a gap between tokens billed and tokens delivered.","Skipping the `Origin` check with cookie auth; the symptom is cross-site WebSocket hijacking found by the first security review."],"when":"Choose WebSockets when the client must send during the server's stream: interrupt or steer a running\ngeneration, voice and other continuous media, interactive agent sessions where a human approves tool\ncalls mid-run, and multi-party rooms. Choose SSE when the server talks and the client listens, which\nis most chat products. Measure connection counts per process, message latency in both directions, time\nto detect dead peers, and reconnect success rate during deploys. Interviewers probe whether you know\nwhy auth is awkward on the handshake, how to make a handler both read and write concurrently, and\nwhat breaks when you scale from one process to many.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes handlers with a reader loop and a cancellable generation task, chooses close codes deliberately, and keeps all sends on one task so frames never interleave.","architect":"Decides which products justify stateful connections, specifies the pub/sub fan-out, sticky routing and reconnect contract, and sets ingress timeouts and ping intervals.","fde":"Gets a customer's voice agent through their corporate proxy and load balancer, which usually means proving the upgrade is allowed and tuning idle timeouts and pings."},"check":{"q":"Product wants a stop button for a chat that currently streams over SSE from four uvicorn\nworkers. Compare adding a cancel endpoint with moving the chat to WebSockets, and say which you pick.","a":"With SSE the stop becomes a `POST /runs/{id}/cancel` that may land on any of the four workers,\nso the generating worker must learn about it through shared state: a cancel flag in Redis checked\nbetween tokens, or a pub/sub channel the generator subscribes to. That is a small amount of code and\nkeeps the stateless HTTP model, auth and resumability. With WebSockets the stop arrives on the same\nsocket as the tokens, so cancellation is local, but you take on handshake auth, origin checks,\nreconnection and resume, ingress configuration and shutdown handling. For a text chat whose only\nclient input is stop, I keep SSE and add the Redis-backed cancel, and I move to WebSockets only if the\nproduct also needs voice or continuous client input."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why can a browser not send an Authorization header on the WebSocket handshake?","options":["The handshake is encrypted before headers are added","The JavaScript WebSocket API offers no way to set request headers, so auth must use cookies, a query token or a first message","WebSocket servers strip all headers after the upgrade","Authorization headers are only valid on POST requests"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The upgrade is a GET the browser issues on your behalf with no header option. This forces the cookie, short-lived query token or post-connect auth-message patterns, each with its own trade-off."},{"q":"In a FastAPI WebSocket handler, why does supporting interruption require a second task?","options":["FastAPI forbids awaiting the model inside a WebSocket route","A single coroutine cannot send more than one frame","One coroutine cannot both await the next incoming message and iterate the model stream at the same time","The event loop processes WebSocket frames on a separate thread"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The handler is one coroutine; whatever it is awaiting, it is not doing the other thing. A reader loop plus a cancellable generation task is the standard shape, with sends kept on one task."},{"q":"What does SSE provide out of the box that WebSockets do not?","options":["Binary frames for audio","Bidirectional messages on one connection","Automatic client reconnection that resends the last event id seen","A ping/pong mechanism for detecting dead peers"],"answer":2,"explanation":"`EventSource` reconnects by itself and sends `Last-Event-ID`; with WebSockets you design session ids, sequence numbers and the reconnect loop. Ping/pong and binary frames are WebSocket features, not SSE."}]},{"key":"fastapi-for-ai/backgroundtasks","sources":["Official docs: FastAPI (Background Tasks)","Official docs: Starlette (Background Tasks)","Official docs: FastAPI (Dependencies with yield)"],"why":"Every LLM request drags follow-up work behind it: persist the prompt, response and token usage for\nobservability and billing, update a rate-limit counter, save an evaluation sample, emit an analytics\nevent, notify a webhook. Doing them inline adds their latency to the response (a database write plus a\nwebhook round trip can be a few hundred milliseconds; illustrative) and couples the user's success to\ntheirs: a slow analytics endpoint should never turn a good answer into a 500.\n\nThe naive escapes each fail. `asyncio.create_task(...)` and forgetting the handle looks right, but the\nevent loop keeps only weak references to tasks, so a task with no strong reference can be garbage\ncollected mid-flight, and its exception is swallowed; the asyncio documentation warns about exactly\nthis. Spawning a thread per request has no limit and loses exceptions the same way. A full job queue\n(Celery, ARQ) is the correct tool for durable work, but it is a broker, a worker fleet and a\nserialisation boundary, which is a lot of machinery for a fifty-millisecond insert.\n\n`BackgroundTasks` is the deliberately small middle: Starlette keeps the task list on the response\nobject and awaits it right after the body has been flushed, so ordering relative to the response is\nguaranteed, sync functions are pushed to the thread pool, async functions are awaited, and nothing new\nhas to be deployed. Its limits are the same facts stated the other way round: the work exists only in\nthis process's memory, so a crash, a deploy or an out-of-memory kill erases it silently.","how":"### Declaring tasks\nAdd a parameter typed `BackgroundTasks` to a path operation or to any dependency; FastAPI injects one\ninstance per request and merges every `add_task(func, *args, **kwargs)` call into a single ordered\nlist. `Response(..., background=BackgroundTask(fn))` is the lower-level Starlette form for custom\nresponses, including `StreamingResponse`.\n\n### When they run\nStarlette's `Response.__call__` sends `http.response.start`, then the body, then, if a background\nobject is attached, `await self.background()`. So tasks start only after the final body bytes have\nbeen handed to the server; the client already has its response. `BackgroundTasks.__call__` runs them\nsequentially in insertion order: an `async def` is awaited on the event loop, a plain `def` is run via\n`run_in_threadpool`. Nothing runs in parallel, so one slow task delays the rest.\n\n### What the server sees\nThe ASGI call for the request has not returned while tasks run. That means uvicorn's graceful shutdown\nwaits for them (up to its shutdown timeout), anything counting in-flight requests counts them, and the\nhandler's resources stay allocated. It also means the work dies with the process: an orchestrator that\nsends SIGKILL after its grace period takes every pending task with it, and nothing recorded that they\nexisted.\n\n### Exceptions\nAn exception in a task propagates out of the application after the response is gone. The server logs\nit (uvicorn prints an \"Exception in ASGI application\" traceback), the client never learns of it, and\nthere is no retry. Wrap each task body in `try/except` with real logging and a metric, and write tasks\nso that running twice is harmless, because the day you move them to a queue they will.\n\n### Interaction with yield dependencies\nA dependency that yields a database session has exit code that must run at some point. Whether that\nteardown runs before or after background tasks has changed across FastAPI versions (0.106 changed the\ndefault so sessions are not held while the response travels the network; later versions added a scope\noption to choose). The only version-proof rule is: never hand a request-scoped resource to a background\ntask. Create the session, client or file handle inside the task.\n\n### Capacity\nSync tasks share the default thread pool (anyio's limiter, 40 threads) with sync endpoints and sync\ndependencies, so a burst of \"cheap\" sync tasks can starve ordinary sync routes. Async tasks share the\nevent loop, so a CPU-heavy task, computing embeddings locally, hashing a large upload, blocks every\nrequest for its duration. Treat a task as part of the request's cost, just paid after the response.","example":"An LLM endpoint that answers immediately and records a trace afterwards. The task opens its own\nsession, guards its own exceptions, and would be harmless if run twice.\n\n```python\nimport logging, time\nfrom fastapi import BackgroundTasks, FastAPI\nfrom pydantic import BaseModel\n\napp, log = FastAPI(), logging.getLogger(\"trace\")\n\nclass Ask(BaseModel):\n    prompt: str\n\nasync def call_model(prompt: str) -> tuple[str, int]:\n    return f\"echo: {prompt}\", 7                      # stand-in: (answer, output_tokens)\n\nasync def record_trace(user_id: str, prompt: str, answer: str, tokens: int, ms: float):\n    try:\n        async with session_factory() as session:     # its OWN session; never the request's\n            session.add(Trace(user_id=user_id, prompt=prompt, answer=answer,\n                              output_tokens=tokens, latency_ms=ms))\n            await session.commit()\n    except Exception:\n        log.exception(\"trace write failed\")          # client already has its answer: log, don't raise\n\n@app.post(\"/answer\")\nasync def answer(body: Ask, bg: BackgroundTasks):\n    t0 = time.perf_counter()\n    text, tokens = await call_model(body.prompt)\n    ms = (time.perf_counter() - t0) * 1000\n    bg.add_task(record_trace, \"user-123\", body.prompt, text, tokens, ms)   # runs after the body is sent\n    return {\"answer\": text}\n\n# session_factory and Trace come from your SQLAlchemy setup (see the async database lesson).\n```","tradeoffs":["Zero infrastructure and in-process execution, but zero durability: the moment losing the work has a cost (billing ledgers, audit records) the trade flips to a queue with persisted jobs.","Latency is hidden from the user, but the worker is still busy; under load, background work competes with new requests for the loop and thread pool, and once tasks take seconds that competition is visible in p99 latency.","Sequential execution per request is simple to reason about, but tasks cannot overlap, so a slow webhook delays the trace write queued behind it.","Exceptions are isolated from the client, which protects the user experience, but the same isolation hides failures unless you add logging and metrics yourself."],"pitfalls":["Running the LLM job itself in the background and returning a job id; the symptom is jobs that vanish after every deploy and a status endpoint with nothing to read, because nothing persisted the job.","Passing the request's yield-dependency session into a task; the symptom is \"session is closed\" or \"connection already returned to pool\" errors, often appearing only after a FastAPI upgrade.","A CPU-heavy sync task; the symptom is latency on unrelated sync endpoints rising with traffic as the 40-thread pool fills up.","A blocking call (sync HTTP client, sync SDK) inside an async task; the symptom is the whole worker freezing for the duration of each task, visible as periodic stalls in every route.","Assuming the client would have noticed a failure; the symptom is silent data loss in traces or counters discovered weeks later during an audit.","Reaching for bare `asyncio.create_task` instead; the symptom is tasks that sometimes never run, because nothing held a reference to them."],"when":"Use `BackgroundTasks` for short, idempotent, loss-tolerant follow-ups: trace and metric writes, cache\nwarming, non-critical notifications, cleanup of temporary files. Do not use it for the LLM call, for\nanything a customer will later ask about, for retries, or for work longer than a few seconds. The\ndecision test is \"if this process dies now, is losing this acceptable?\" Measure task duration, failure\nrate from your own logging, and thread-pool saturation. In interviews the key distinction to articulate\nis after-response versus durable: when it runs, where it runs, and what happens on a crash.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Queues trace writes and counters after the response, gives each task its own resources and exception handling, and knows the thread-pool and event-loop cost of each.","architect":"Draws the line between loss-tolerant follow-ups and durable jobs, and mandates a queue for anything with financial or audit consequences.","fde":"Spots a customer's \"we run the summarisation in a BackgroundTask\" design and explains why their jobs disappear on deploys before it becomes a support escalation."},"check":{"q":"A team writes the per-request billing ledger entry in a `BackgroundTasks` function to keep\nlatency low. What is wrong with that, and what would you do instead?","a":"A ledger entry is money, and `BackgroundTasks` offers no durability: a crash, SIGKILL after the\nshutdown grace period or an unhandled exception loses the entry silently, and the client has already\nreceived a success. Either write the entry synchronously in the same transaction as the request's own\nstate, which costs a few milliseconds and is the simplest correct option, or record the intent\ndurably before responding and process it asynchronously: insert an outbox row in that transaction and\nlet a worker or queue consumer post it, with idempotency keyed by request id so retries never\ndouble-charge. Keep `BackgroundTasks` for the non-financial trace and metrics writes."},"quiz":[{"q":"When exactly does a function added with BackgroundTasks start running?","options":["Immediately, in parallel with the handler, on a separate thread","After the response body has been sent to the client, inside the same process and request lifecycle","When the event loop is idle, on a best-effort basis","On the next request to the same worker"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Starlette awaits the background object right after flushing the body, so the client already has its response but the ASGI call is still running until the tasks finish."},{"q":"What happens when a background task raises an exception?","options":["The client receives a 500 response","FastAPI retries the task up to three times","The server logs the traceback, the client is unaffected, and the work is simply lost","The exception is delivered to the next request on that connection"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The response is already sent, so there is no status code to change and no retry machinery. Your own try/except with logging is the only visibility you get."},{"q":"Which of these is an appropriate use of BackgroundTasks?","options":["Running a three-minute batch summarisation and returning a job id","Capturing a card payment after responding with \"order confirmed\"","Posting a non-critical analytics event about the completed request","Writing the invoice record that finance reconciles against"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The test is whether losing the work on a crash is acceptable. An analytics event is; a payment, an invoice or a multi-minute job need a durable queue with persisted job state and retries."}]},{"key":"fastapi-for-ai/job-queues-and-durable-workers","sources":["Official docs: Celery","Official docs: ARQ","Kleppmann, 2017 — Designing Data-Intensive Applications"],"why":"Three constraints kill long HTTP requests. Intermediaries cut connections that run or idle too long,\ncommonly after tens of seconds by default (vendor-dependent), so a five-minute corpus summarisation\nnever returns. Clients retry on timeout, so the work runs twice and the second run races the first.\nAnd a deploy, crash or scale-down kills in-flight work with nothing to say it existed. The AI shape of\nthe problem is worse than classic web work because a single job can mean thousands of model calls at\nreal cost, bounded by provider rate limits rather than by your CPUs.\n\n`BackgroundTasks` does not help: it runs after the response in the same process and vanishes with it.\nHolding the connection open with huge timeouts hides the symptom and still loses work on restart. The\nbackend-fundamentals lesson on durable workers covers the theory, leases, acknowledgements and\nvisibility timeouts; this lesson is the FastAPI-facing shape of it. The idea is to split \"accept\" from\n\"do\": the API validates, persists a job record, enqueues a small message, and hands back a handle; a\nseparate worker fleet claims jobs, does the work with periodic progress writes, and records the result;\nthe client polls or subscribes for the outcome. Each half scales and fails independently, and the job\nrecord, not the HTTP connection, is the source of truth.","how":"### The contract between API and client\n1. `POST /jobs`: validate input fully, because this is the last moment you can return a 4xx. Insert a\n   job row in your own database (`id`, `owner`, `status=queued`, `created_at`, the idempotency key if\n   the client sent one). Enqueue a message carrying the job id and small parameters, never the payload\n   itself; a 50 MB document goes to object storage and the message carries its key. Return `202` with\n   `{\"job_id\": ..., \"status_url\": ...}` and a `Location` header.\n2. `GET /jobs/{id}`: check the caller owns the job (answer 404, not 403, so ids cannot be probed).\n   Return `status` (`queued`, `running`, `succeeded`, `failed`, `cancelled`), `progress`, `result` or a\n   `result_url`, a sanitised `error`, and timestamps. Add `Retry-After` so well-behaved clients back\n   off.\n3. The worker claims a job, writes `running` with its worker id and start time, performs the work with\n   progress updates, writes the result and a terminal status. Terminal states are immutable.\n4. Optionally push: an SSE endpoint that tails the job row, or a webhook on completion.\n\nWhy your own row and not the broker's result backend: result backends are ephemeral (TTL-bound), carry\nno ownership or audit metadata, and couple your API to the queue library. The row also absorbs a\nclassic race: write the row and commit *before* enqueueing; if the enqueue fails, a sweeper re-enqueues\nrows stuck in `queued`. Enqueueing first can produce a worker that finds no row. The outbox pattern\nmakes this airtight when you need it.\n\n### Celery\nTasks are plain functions decorated with `@app.task`; `task.delay(...)` or `apply_async(args,\ncountdown=..., queue=...)` enqueues to RabbitMQ, Redis or SQS. The default worker pool is prefork, one\nOS process per concurrency slot, which suits CPU-bound and sync code. Reliability is opt-in: by\ndefault a message is acknowledged when received, so a worker crash loses it; set `acks_late=True` and\n`task_reject_on_worker_lost=True` for at-least-once. With the Redis broker an unacknowledged message is\nredelivered after `visibility_timeout`, which defaults to one hour, so any job longer than that runs\ntwice unless you raise it. Retries come from `autoretry_for`, `retry_backoff`, `max_retries` and\n`self.retry()`; `soft_time_limit` and `time_limit` bound runaway tasks. Celery has no native asyncio\ntask body: calling an async SDK means `asyncio.run(...)` inside the task or using the sync client.\nBeat schedules periodic jobs, Flower monitors, and the canvas primitives (chains, groups, chords) express\nfan-out such as \"embed every chunk, then build the index\".\n\n### ARQ\nTasks are `async def` functions taking a `ctx` dict first. A `WorkerSettings` class lists `functions`,\n`redis_settings`, `max_jobs` (concurrent jobs per worker process), `job_timeout`, `max_tries`,\n`keep_result` and `on_startup`/`on_shutdown` hooks where you create shared clients (an `httpx` client,\na database pool) into `ctx`. Enqueue with `await pool.enqueue_job(\"name\", *args, _job_id=...)`; a job\nid that already exists is not enqueued again, which gives you deduplication for free. Retries are\nrequested by raising `arq.Retry(defer=seconds)`. One worker process holds `max_jobs` concurrent\ncoroutines, so a single process can keep many LLM calls in flight, which is exactly the shape of\nIO-bound AI work. It is Redis-only and small, with no canvas, routing or RabbitMQ.\n\n### Choosing\nPick Celery when the organisation already runs it, when you need RabbitMQ, multiple queues with\npriorities, Beat schedules or canvas workflows, or when jobs are CPU-bound. Pick ARQ when the codebase\nis asyncio, Redis is already present, jobs are IO-bound model calls, and a small operational surface\nmatters. Either way the body must be idempotent because delivery is at-least-once: check the job's\nstatus before acting, write results with upserts keyed by job id, and pass idempotency keys to\nexternal calls. For multi-step agent runs that need checkpoints and resumption, a workflow engine\nis the next step up; see the durable workflow lesson.\n\n### Backpressure and capacity\nQueue depth is your backpressure signal: export it, cap in-flight jobs per tenant, and reject new jobs\nwith `429` or `503` when depth times average duration exceeds what you promised. Use separate queues\nfor cheap interactive jobs and expensive batch jobs so one never starves the other. Size worker\nconcurrency by provider rate limits and memory, not CPU.","example":"The FastAPI side of the contract with an ARQ worker. The job row is committed before enqueueing, the\nstatus endpoint hides other users' jobs, and the worker checks status first so a redelivered job is a\nno-op. `db` stands for your async data-access layer.\n\n```python\nimport uuid\nfrom fastapi import FastAPI, HTTPException, Request, Response\nfrom pydantic import BaseModel\n\napp = FastAPI()                       # lifespan creates app.state.arq = await create_pool(RedisSettings())\n\nclass Req(BaseModel):\n    user_id: str\n    corpus_id: str\n\n@app.post(\"/jobs/summaries\", status_code=202)\nasync def create(body: Req, request: Request, response: Response):\n    job_id = str(uuid.uuid4())\n    await db.create_job(job_id, owner=body.user_id, status=\"queued\")        # commit BEFORE enqueue\n    await request.app.state.arq.enqueue_job(\"summarize_corpus\", job_id, body.corpus_id, _job_id=job_id)\n    response.headers[\"Location\"] = f\"/jobs/{job_id}\"\n    return {\"job_id\": job_id, \"status_url\": f\"/jobs/{job_id}\"}\n\n@app.get(\"/jobs/{job_id}\")\nasync def status(job_id: str, user_id: str, response: Response):\n    job = await db.get_job(job_id)\n    if job is None or job.owner != user_id:\n        raise HTTPException(404)                                            # 404, not 403: don't leak ids\n    if job.status in (\"queued\", \"running\"):\n        response.headers[\"Retry-After\"] = \"5\"\n    return {\"status\": job.status, \"progress\": job.progress, \"result_url\": job.result_url, \"error\": job.error}\n\n# worker.py  — run with:  arq worker.WorkerSettings\nfrom arq.connections import RedisSettings\n\nasync def summarize_corpus(ctx, job_id: str, corpus_id: str):\n    if await db.job_status(job_id) == \"succeeded\":                          # at-least-once: redelivery is a no-op\n        return\n    await db.set_status(job_id, \"running\")\n    try:\n        docs = await db.docs(corpus_id)\n        for i, doc in enumerate(docs, 1):\n            await db.upsert_summary(doc.id, await ctx[\"llm\"].summarize(doc))   # idempotent write per doc\n            await db.set_progress(job_id, i, len(docs))\n        await db.set_status(job_id, \"succeeded\", result_url=f\"/corpora/{corpus_id}/summaries\")\n    except Exception as exc:\n        await db.set_status(job_id, \"failed\", error=type(exc).__name__); raise   # raise so ARQ counts the try\n\nclass WorkerSettings:\n    functions = [summarize_corpus]\n    redis_settings = RedisSettings()\n    max_jobs, job_timeout, max_tries = 10, 1800, 3   # concurrency bound by LLM rate limits, not CPU\n```","tradeoffs":["Durability and horizontal scaling of workers, but a second deployable, a broker to operate, and a serialisation boundary; for follow-ups measured in milliseconds that can be lost, `BackgroundTasks` is cheaper.","At-least-once delivery means no job is lost, but every job can run twice, so idempotency is a requirement rather than a nicety; the flip is at-most-once (Celery's default early ack), which is only acceptable for work you would rather drop than repeat.","Polling a status endpoint is simple and stateless, but at scale it becomes your highest-QPS route; once clients number in the thousands, push via SSE or webhooks pays for itself.","Celery's breadth (brokers, routing, Beat, canvas) buys flexibility at the price of sync task bodies and more configuration; ARQ's asyncio model fits LLM IO perfectly but stops at Redis and a single queue abstraction."],"pitfalls":["Enqueueing before the job row is committed; the symptom is workers logging \"job not found\" and retrying until the row appears, or never.","Stuffing documents into the message; the symptom is Redis memory spikes and slow enqueues; pass object-storage keys instead.","Keeping Celery's default early acknowledgement; the symptom is jobs stuck in `running` forever after a worker is OOM-killed.","A Redis `visibility_timeout` shorter than the longest job; the symptom is the same job id appearing twice in worker logs and doubled model spend.","Serving status from the broker's result backend; the symptom is `404` for jobs older than the result TTL, typically an hour.","No ownership check on the status route; the symptom is a security finding that job ids, often sequential, expose other tenants' results.","Clients polling in tight loops; the symptom is the status endpoint dominating request volume; send `Retry-After` and document a backoff.","Letting a retried job overwrite a terminal state; the symptom is a job flipping from `succeeded` back to `running` and confusing every dashboard."],"when":"Use a queue when work outlives a reasonable HTTP timeout, must survive restarts, needs retries, or\nshould be throttled against provider rate limits independently of API traffic: corpus ingestion and\nembedding, batch evaluation runs, scheduled re-summarisation, long agent tasks. Do not use it for\ninteractive single-turn chat, where streaming is the right answer, or for sub-second follow-ups.\nMeasure queue depth, time-in-queue, job duration percentiles, retry and duplicate rates, and status\npolls per job. In interviews, be ready to draw the 202-plus-status contract, explain why the job row\nbeats the result backend, and say what at-least-once forces on job bodies.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes idempotent task bodies, the enqueue-after-commit path, and the status route with ownership checks and `Retry-After`, and wires shared clients into worker startup hooks.","architect":"Chooses Celery versus ARQ versus a workflow engine, defines queue separation, per-tenant limits and backpressure policy, and decides where job state lives.","fde":"Migrates a customer's \"run it in a background task\" prototype to a queue when deploys start losing jobs, and tunes worker concurrency against their provider rate limits."},"check":{"q":"A worker crashes halfway through summarising 1,000 documents. Walk through what happens with\nCelery at default settings, with `acks_late=True`, and what your job code must do in each case.","a":"At default settings Celery acknowledges the message on receipt, so after the crash the broker\nconsiders it done: the job row stays `running` forever, 600 summaries are missing, and nobody retries.\nA sweeper that marks stale `running` jobs as failed is the minimum mitigation. With `acks_late=True`\n(and `task_reject_on_worker_lost`) the message is redelivered after the visibility timeout, so the\njob runs again from the start on another worker. For that to be correct, the task must be idempotent:\ncheck the job status first, skip documents that already have a summary (upsert keyed by document id),\nand update progress monotonically, so the second run does the remaining 600 and marks success. In both\ncases the row, not the broker, is what the status endpoint and the on-call engineer read."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why does the API return 202 with a job id instead of holding the connection until the work finishes?","options":["Because FastAPI cannot keep a connection open for more than thirty seconds","Because intermediaries cut long connections, clients retry and duplicate work, and in-flight work dies with the process, whereas a persisted job survives all three","Because 202 responses are cached by browsers","Because workers cannot write HTTP responses"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The job record plus a status endpoint decouples completion from the connection. The connection can drop, the client can retry safely, and the worker can be restarted without losing the job."},{"q":"Why must a job function check the job's status before doing its work?","options":["To update the job's `started_at` timestamp","Because ARQ requires it for the result to be stored","Because delivery is at-least-once, so the same job can be redelivered after a crash or timeout and must be a no-op if already done","To obtain a lock on the Redis key"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Both Celery with late acks and ARQ redeliver jobs whose worker died. Reading the status first and using idempotent writes turns a duplicate delivery into a harmless resume rather than double work."},{"q":"Which situation most favours ARQ over Celery?","options":["The team needs RabbitMQ with priority queues and Beat schedules","An asyncio codebase on Redis whose jobs are mostly concurrent IO-bound model calls","Jobs are CPU-bound image preprocessing that must use every core","The organisation already runs Celery for other services"],"answer":1,"explanation":"ARQ's async task bodies let one process keep many model calls in flight. Celery's prefork pool and broker breadth are the better fit for CPU-bound work, RabbitMQ features, and existing Celery estates."}]},{"key":"fastapi-for-ai/async-database-access-and-connection-pooling","sources":["Official docs: SQLAlchemy (Asyncio Support; Connection Pooling)","Official docs: PostgreSQL (Connections and Authentication)","Official docs: PgBouncer"],"why":"Two separate problems hide behind \"talk to the database from FastAPI\". The first is blocking. A\nsynchronous driver call such as psycopg2's `cursor.execute()` inside an `async def` endpoint freezes\nthe event loop for the query's duration. At 20 ms per query and fifty concurrent requests (illustrative)\nnothing else runs, including health checks, so the symptom is a readiness probe failing exactly when\ntraffic is highest. FastAPI offers two correct escapes: a plain `def` endpoint, which it runs on a\nworker thread, or an async driver that yields to the loop while the server works. The thread-pool\nroute caps concurrency at the pool size (anyio's default limiter is 40) and makes mixing a database\nread with an awaited model call awkward, which is why AI services usually take the async-driver route.\n\nThe second problem is connections. Every Postgres connection is a backend process on the server with\nits own memory (a few megabytes each, order of magnitude), and establishing one means a TCP handshake,\nusually TLS, and authentication, which together cost milliseconds to tens of milliseconds. The server\nalso refuses connections beyond `max_connections`, whose default is 100. A service that opens a\nconnection per request pays the handshake every time and, under a burst, storms the server into\n\"too many clients already\". A pool solves both: keep a bounded set of connections open per process,\nlend one out per unit of work, take it back when done. But a pool per process multiplied by many\nprocesses and worker fleets is itself a capacity problem, and that arithmetic, not query speed, is what\nusually bites first.","how":"### The driver layer\nSQLAlchemy URLs name the driver: `postgresql+asyncpg://` (binary protocol, fastest, its own API) or\n`postgresql+psycopg://` (psycopg 3 with async support, DB-API shaped); `sqlite+aiosqlite://` for tests.\nSQLAlchemy's asyncio extension keeps its synchronous core and bridges to the async driver with\ngreenlets: ORM code runs inside a greenlet and switches out whenever the driver would block. The\nconsequence is that any implicit IO where you cannot `await`, such as accessing a lazy relationship or\nan attribute expired by `commit()`, raises `MissingGreenlet`. So async sessions use eager loading\n(`selectinload`, `joinedload`) and `expire_on_commit=False`.\n\n### Pool mechanics\n`create_async_engine(url, pool_size=10, max_overflow=5, pool_timeout=30, pool_recycle=1800,\npool_pre_ping=True)` configures the per-process pool. `pool_size` connections persist; up to\n`max_overflow` extra are opened on demand and closed when returned (SQLAlchemy's defaults are 5 and\n10). A checkout that waits longer than `pool_timeout` raises the famous `QueuePool limit of size N\noverflow M reached, connection timed out`. `pool_pre_ping` sends a trivial query on checkout so a\nconnection killed by a failover is replaced instead of handed to you, at the cost of one round trip.\n`pool_recycle` closes connections older than the given seconds, because cloud databases and network\nappliances silently drop idle ones. A session checks a connection out on its first statement and\nreturns it on `commit()`, `rollback()` or `close()`.\n\n### The per-request session\nThe idiomatic dependency is a `yield` that opens an `AsyncSession` from an `async_sessionmaker` and\ncloses it afterwards. The trap is what happens between: a session held open across\n`await call_model(...)` holds its connection for the whole model call. With 15 connections per process\nand an average 8-second model call, the process can serve at most about two requests per second before\ncheckouts time out, while the database sits idle. The fix is structural: read what you need, release\nthe connection (`commit()` or `close()`), call the model, then open a short second session to write.\n\n### Process and loop boundaries\nThe engine and its pool belong to one process and one event loop. Create them in the lifespan handler\nrather than at import time: with gunicorn `--preload` a pool created before the fork is shared by\nevery child and connections get corrupted, and asyncpg connections are bound to the loop that made\nthem, which is also why tests that spin up their own loop break with import-time engines. Call\n`engine.dispose()` on shutdown.\n\n### Capacity arithmetic\nFour API processes with `pool_size=10, max_overflow=5` can hold 60 connections. Three ARQ workers with\na pool of 10 add 30. A migration job and a few operator shells add a handful. Postgres keeps a few\nconnections reserved for superusers (`superuser_reserved_connections`, 3 by default), so the budget\nis 97, and 60 + 30 + 5 = 95 fits, barely. Add one replica of either tier and it does not. That is when\nPgBouncer enters: application pools connect to PgBouncer, which multiplexes them onto a small server\npool. In transaction pooling mode a server connection is assigned per transaction, so anything that is\nsession-scoped breaks: `SET` session variables, advisory locks held across transactions,\n`LISTEN/NOTIFY`, and prepared statements. asyncpg prepares statements implicitly, so with PgBouncer in\ntransaction mode set `statement_cache_size=0` in `connect_args`, or rely on a PgBouncer version with\nprotocol-level prepared-statement support.\n\n### Concurrency inside one request\nA session is not safe for concurrent use. `asyncio.gather(session.execute(a), session.execute(b))`\nfails with an error about concurrent operations on one session. Run statements sequentially, or give\neach task its own session, which also means its own connection.","example":"Engine and session factory created in the lifespan, a standard per-request dependency for ordinary\nroutes, and a chat endpoint that returns its connection before the slow model call and opens a short\nsecond session for the write.\n\n```python\nfrom contextlib import asynccontextmanager\nfrom fastapi import Depends, FastAPI, Request\nfrom sqlalchemy import select\nfrom sqlalchemy.ext.asyncio import AsyncSession, async_sessionmaker, create_async_engine\n\n@asynccontextmanager\nasync def lifespan(app: FastAPI):\n    app.state.engine = create_async_engine(\n        \"postgresql+asyncpg://app:pw@db/app\",\n        pool_size=10, max_overflow=5, pool_timeout=10, pool_recycle=1800, pool_pre_ping=True,\n    )                                         # per process: 4 uvicorn workers -> up to 60 connections\n    app.state.sessions = async_sessionmaker(app.state.engine, expire_on_commit=False)\n    yield\n    await app.state.engine.dispose()\n\napp = FastAPI(lifespan=lifespan)\n\nasync def get_session(request: Request):      # for ordinary CRUD routes: one session per request\n    async with request.app.state.sessions() as session:\n        yield session\n\n@app.post(\"/chat/{conv_id}\")\nasync def chat(conv_id: int, body: Ask, request: Request):\n    sessions = request.app.state.sessions\n    async with sessions() as s:               # session 1: read, then RELEASE the connection\n        history = (await s.scalars(select(Message).where(Message.conv_id == conv_id)\n                                   .order_by(Message.id))).all()\n\n    answer = await call_model(body.prompt, history)   # seconds; holds no database connection\n\n    async with sessions() as s:               # session 2: short write\n        s.add(Message(conv_id=conv_id, role=\"assistant\", content=answer))\n        await s.commit()\n    return {\"answer\": answer}\n\n# Ask, Message and call_model are the Pydantic model, ORM model and model client from the other lessons.\n```","tradeoffs":["An async driver lets one process keep hundreds of requests in flight, but every database touch must be awaited and the ORM's implicit lazy loading is gone; for a simple CRUD service with a team new to asyncio, `def` endpoints with a sync driver are less error-prone.","Larger pools cut checkout waits, but they multiply across processes against a fixed `max_connections`; once the total exceeds the server, the answer is PgBouncer, not bigger pools.","`pool_pre_ping` costs a round trip per checkout but eliminates \"server closed the connection unexpectedly\" after failovers; if every microsecond matters and `pool_recycle` is short, turn it off.","Transaction-mode PgBouncer multiplexes thousands of client connections, but forbids session state and complicates prepared statements; session mode keeps the semantics and gives back most of the savings."],"pitfalls":["Holding a session across a model or HTTP call; the symptom is `QueuePool limit ... reached` under modest traffic while database CPU is near idle.","A sync driver inside `async def`; the symptom is every route, including `/health`, stalling together whenever database-heavy traffic arrives.","Lazy loading or `expire_on_commit=True` in async sessions; the symptom is `MissingGreenlet` raised from an innocent attribute access.","Creating the engine at import time under gunicorn preload or in tests; the symptom is asyncpg errors such as \"another operation is in progress\" or \"attached to a different loop\".","Sharing one session across `asyncio.gather`; the symptom is an error about concurrent operations on the session, or silently interleaved transactions.","asyncpg behind transaction-mode PgBouncer with the statement cache on; the symptom is `prepared statement \"__asyncpg_stmt_N__\" does not exist` or \"already exists\" errors under load."],"when":"Use the async driver path for any FastAPI service that mixes database access with awaited model or\nHTTP calls, which is nearly every AI backend. Stay on sync drivers in `def` endpoints for small\ninternal tools or when the team has no asyncio experience. Do the capacity arithmetic before the first\nload test: processes, pools, workers, reserved connections, and whether PgBouncer is needed. Measure\ncheckout wait time, pool saturation, connection count on the server, and the time each request holds a\nconnection. In interviews, expect to compute the pool budget, explain why holding a connection across\na model call is the classic failure, and describe what transaction pooling breaks.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Creates the engine in the lifespan, writes the session dependency, structures handlers so no connection is held across a model call, and tunes pool sizes from measured checkout waits.","architect":"Owns the connection budget across API processes, workers and jobs, decides when PgBouncer is introduced and in which mode, and sets the no-session-across-LLM-call rule as policy.","fde":"Diagnoses a customer's \"database is slow\" as pool exhaustion caused by long model calls, and proves it with pool metrics against an idle database."},"check":{"q":"A service runs 6 uvicorn workers, each with `pool_size=10, max_overflow=10`, against a Postgres\nwith default `max_connections`. Under load users see connection errors. Explain and fix it.","a":"The arithmetic is 6 × (10 + 10) = 120 possible connections against a server allowing 100, of\nwhich a few are reserved for superusers, so under load the overflow connections are refused with \"too\nmany clients already\" and surface as 500s. Short-term, cut the per-process pool so the total fits, for\nexample `pool_size=8, max_overflow=4` for 72, leaving headroom for workers and migrations. Then check\nwhy the pool fills: if handlers hold sessions across model calls, restructuring them to release before\nthe slow call will drop real usage far below the cap. If the fleet must grow beyond the budget, put\nPgBouncer in front in transaction mode, set asyncpg's `statement_cache_size=0`, and audit for\nsession-scoped features such as `SET` and advisory locks that transaction pooling breaks."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why does holding a session open across an awaited model call hurt, even though the code is fully async?","options":["The event loop is blocked while the model responds","The session keeps a pooled connection checked out for the whole call, so the pool empties while the database does nothing","asyncpg closes idle connections after one second","SQLAlchemy serialises all sessions in a process"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Async keeps the loop free, but the connection is a scarce resource held for seconds per request. Fifteen connections and eight-second calls cap a process at roughly two requests per second."},{"q":"What does a `MissingGreenlet` error in an async SQLAlchemy app indicate?","options":["The greenlet package is not installed","The engine was created on a different event loop","Implicit database IO was triggered outside an awaitable context, typically a lazy load or an attribute expired by commit","The pool has been exhausted"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The async bridge can only switch to the driver inside an awaited operation. Lazy relationship access and post-commit refresh try to query synchronously, so use eager loading and `expire_on_commit=False`."},{"q":"Which feature breaks when you put asyncpg behind PgBouncer in transaction pooling mode without changes?","options":["SELECT statements with JOINs","Implicit prepared statements, because the server connection can change between transactions","Connection pooling in SQLAlchemy, which must be disabled","TLS between the application and the database"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Transaction mode hands out a different server connection per transaction, so a statement prepared on one may not exist on the next. Set `statement_cache_size=0` or use protocol-level prepared statement support."}]},{"key":"fastapi-for-ai/sqlalchemy-sqlmodel","sources":["Official docs: SQLAlchemy 2.0 (Unified Tutorial; ORM Querying Guide)","Official docs: SQLModel"],"why":"AI backends carry a lot of relational state: conversations and messages with JSON tool-call payloads,\njobs and their progress, documents and chunks with embeddings, evaluation runs and scores. Writing SQL\nstrings by hand gets three things wrong at once. Interpolated values invite injection; a column list\nrepeated in a Pydantic model, an INSERT and a SELECT drifts out of sync; and loading related rows\n(every message of every conversation) is coded as a loop of queries that nobody notices until the\ntable is large.\n\nSQLAlchemy's Core answers the first two with an expression language that compiles to parameterised SQL\nfor whichever dialect you run, and the ORM answers the third with relationships, loading strategies\nand a unit of work that orders INSERTs and UPDATEs by foreign-key dependency. Its declarative models\nalso become the input to Alembic, so schema changes are generated from code rather than typed twice.\n\nSQLModel exists because FastAPI already forces a Pydantic model for every request and response, and\nduplicating those fields in a SQLAlchemy model is tedious and error-prone. By making a class\nsimultaneously a Pydantic model and a SQLAlchemy declarative model, SQLModel collapses the duplication\nand keeps validation, OpenAPI docs and the table definition in one place. The naive alternative, plain\nPydantic plus raw asyncpg, works and is fast, but you give up autogenerated migrations, relationship\nloading and the identity map, and you end up rebuilding a worse ORM by hand.","how":"### Core versus ORM\nCore is `Table`, `select()`, `insert()`, `update()` and an `Engine` whose connections execute compiled\nstatements with bound parameters. The ORM sits on top: a `DeclarativeBase` subclass with\n`id: Mapped[int] = mapped_column(primary_key=True)` style attributes, and a `Session` that holds an\nidentity map (one Python object per row per session) and a unit of work. `session.add()` registers an\nobject, `flush()` emits SQL in dependency order, `commit()` flushes, commits and by default *expires*\nevery loaded instance so the next attribute access reloads from the database. That default,\n`expire_on_commit=True`, is the trap in async code: the reload is implicit IO and raises\n`MissingGreenlet`, so async sessions are created with `expire_on_commit=False`.\n\nThe 2.0 query style is `session.execute(select(Message).where(...))`, which returns rows, or\n`session.scalars(...)`, which returns objects; the legacy `session.query()` still works but is not\nwhere new features land. Relationship loading is lazy by default, which is the N+1 problem waiting to\nhappen; `selectinload()` issues one extra `IN (...)` query per relationship, `joinedload()` folds it into\na JOIN, and `raiseload()` turns accidental lazy loads into errors during tests. JSON columns store tool\ncalls and metadata; the `pgvector` package adds a `Vector(dim)` column type for embeddings.\n\n### What SQLModel adds\n`class Message(SQLModel, table=True)` declares fields with ordinary type annotations; each field is a\nPydantic field *and* a column. `Field(primary_key=True, index=True, foreign_key=\"conversation.id\",\nsa_column=Column(JSON))` reaches SQLAlchemy column options, and `Relationship(back_populates=...)`\nmaps relationships. A `SQLModel` subclass *without* `table=True` is a pure Pydantic schema, so the\nidiom is a shared base with the common fields, a table model that adds `id` and timestamps, and\n`Create`/`Read` schemas that add or omit fields for the API. `sqlmodel.Session` and the async session\nin `sqlmodel.ext.asyncio.session` are SQLAlchemy sessions with a `session.exec(select(...))` method\nthat returns typed scalars directly. Two sharp edges are documented: table models do not run Pydantic\nvalidation when instantiated, so validate incoming data through a non-table schema and convert with\n`Message.model_validate(payload, update={...})`; and SQLModel tracks SQLAlchemy with some lag, so a\nfeature missing from SQLModel's surface is usually reachable through `sa_column` or `sa_relationship`\nrather than absent. Because its metadata *is* SQLAlchemy metadata, Alembic works unchanged.\n\n### Choosing between them\nSQLModel pays off when the API payload and the row look alike and the project is small enough that one\nset of classes is a feature. Plain SQLAlchemy 2.0 pays off when storage and API shapes differ\n(denormalised read models, columns the client must never see), when you use advanced mapping (hybrid\nproperties, polymorphism, composite types), or when the team already knows it. For hot paths such as\nbulk-inserting a million chunks, drop to Core `insert()` with a list of dicts or to the driver's bulk\ncopy, inside the same engine.","example":"A SQLModel schema for conversations and messages with the table/schema split, and two sync endpoints.\nSync endpoints run on the thread pool, which is fine for CRUD; the async session is a drop-in for the\npattern in the async database lesson.\n\n```python\nfrom datetime import datetime, timezone\nfrom typing import Optional\nfrom fastapi import Depends, FastAPI\nfrom sqlalchemy import JSON, Column\nfrom sqlmodel import Field, Relationship, Session, SQLModel, create_engine, select\n\nclass Conversation(SQLModel, table=True):\n    id: Optional[int] = Field(default=None, primary_key=True)\n    user_id: str = Field(index=True)\n    messages: list[\"Message\"] = Relationship(back_populates=\"conversation\")\n\nclass MessageBase(SQLModel):                        # fields the API and the table agree on\n    role: str\n    content: str\n    tool_calls: Optional[dict] = Field(default=None, sa_column=Column(JSON))\n\nclass Message(MessageBase, table=True):\n    id: Optional[int] = Field(default=None, primary_key=True)\n    conversation_id: int = Field(foreign_key=\"conversation.id\", index=True)\n    created_at: datetime = Field(default_factory=lambda: datetime.now(timezone.utc))\n    conversation: Conversation = Relationship(back_populates=\"messages\")\n\nclass MessageCreate(MessageBase): pass               # clients may not set id or timestamps\nclass MessageRead(MessageBase):\n    id: int\n    created_at: datetime\n\nengine = create_engine(\"postgresql+psycopg://app:pw@db/app\", pool_pre_ping=True)\napp = FastAPI()\n\ndef get_session():\n    with Session(engine) as session:\n        yield session\n\n@app.post(\"/conversations/{conv_id}/messages\", response_model=MessageRead)\ndef add_message(conv_id: int, body: MessageCreate, session: Session = Depends(get_session)):\n    msg = Message.model_validate(body, update={\"conversation_id\": conv_id})   # validated via the schema\n    session.add(msg); session.commit(); session.refresh(msg)\n    return msg\n\n@app.get(\"/conversations/{conv_id}/messages\", response_model=list[MessageRead])\ndef list_messages(conv_id: int, session: Session = Depends(get_session)):\n    stmt = select(Message).where(Message.conversation_id == conv_id).order_by(Message.id)\n    return session.exec(stmt).all()\n```","tradeoffs":["The ORM's identity map and unit of work remove whole classes of bugs, but they add per-object overhead; for bulk ingestion of chunks the flip point is a few thousand rows, where Core inserts or driver-level copy are an order of magnitude faster.","SQLModel's single class per entity removes duplication, but couples the API contract to the table; the moment a client must not see a column or a response needs a computed shape, you are back to separate schemas and have lost the benefit.","Lazy loading makes simple code simple, but each relationship access is a query; explicit `selectinload` is more typing and is the only way the query count stays flat as data grows.","Typed 2.0-style models give IDE and type-checker support, but mixing them with legacy `Query` code in one codebase produces confusing deprecation warnings and inconsistent typing."],"pitfalls":["`expire_on_commit` left at its default in async sessions; the symptom is `MissingGreenlet` on the first attribute read after `commit()`.","Lazy relationship access inside a loop; the symptom is one query plus one per row in the SQL log, and a list endpoint whose latency grows linearly with conversation length.","Using the table model as the request body; the symptom is clients setting `id` or `created_at`, and inputs that bypass validation because table models skip it.","A module-level session shared across requests; the symptom is cross-request data leaks and \"Session is already flushing\" or stale-object errors under concurrency.","Mutating a JSON column in place (`msg.tool_calls[\"x\"] = 1`); the symptom is a commit that writes nothing, because SQLAlchemy only detects reassignment unless the column uses a mutable wrapper.","Foreign-key columns without an index; the symptom is a sequential scan on every \"messages for conversation\" query, since Postgres does not index foreign keys automatically."],"when":"Reach for SQLAlchemy whenever a FastAPI service owns relational data, which is most of them; choose\nSQLModel for small to medium services whose API and storage shapes align and whose team wants one set\nof models, and plain SQLAlchemy 2.0 when the domain or the mapping gets complex. Measure query counts\nper request (the N+1 detector), time in session, and bulk-insert throughput. In day-to-day work the\nrecurring tasks are defining the Create/Read/table split, choosing loading strategies, and keeping\nmodels and migrations in step. In interviews, be ready to explain identity map and unit of work,\nwhy `expire_on_commit` matters in async code, and what SQLModel actually is underneath.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Defines models and schemas, picks loading strategies per endpoint, and knows when to drop to Core or the driver for bulk writes of chunks and embeddings.","architect":"Decides SQLModel versus plain SQLAlchemy for the codebase, sets the rule that API schemas and table models are separate classes, and owns the data model that migrations evolve.","fde":"Reads a customer's existing SQLAlchemy models to wire an AI feature into their schema without breaking their migrations or their ORM conventions."},"check":{"q":"A list endpoint returning conversations with their messages is fast in development and takes\nseconds in production. What is happening and how do you fix it without changing the response?","a":"Almost certainly N+1 loading: the query fetches the conversations, and then the response\nserialiser touches `conversation.messages` for each one, which lazily issues one query per\nconversation. In development there are five conversations; in production there are five hundred, so\nthe endpoint runs five hundred and one queries. Confirm by logging SQL or counting statements in a\ntest. Fix it by adding `options(selectinload(Conversation.messages))` to the select, which loads all\nmessages in one extra `IN` query, or `joinedload` if the result set is small. In async code this is\nalso the only correct option, because the lazy load would raise `MissingGreenlet`. Add `raiseload` in\ntests so the regression cannot return."},"quiz":[{"q":"What is the one thing SQLModel adds on top of SQLAlchemy?","options":["A faster asynchronous driver for PostgreSQL","Classes that are simultaneously Pydantic models and SQLAlchemy declarative models, so one definition serves validation, docs and the table","Automatic schema migrations without Alembic","A query cache shared across requests"],"answer":1,"explanation":"SQLModel is a thin layer that merges the two class systems. Its sessions, engine, metadata and migrations are all SQLAlchemy's; what it buys is the removal of duplicated field definitions."},{"q":"Why define a separate MessageCreate schema instead of accepting the Message table model as the request body?","options":["FastAPI cannot generate OpenAPI docs from table models","Table models cannot be used with `response_model`","Clients could set server-owned fields such as id and timestamps, and table models skip Pydantic validation on instantiation","Table models are not JSON-serialisable"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The non-table schema is where validation happens and where the client-facing contract lives. The table model is converted from it with `model_validate(..., update=...)` once the server adds its fields."},{"q":"In an async session with the default expire_on_commit, what happens when you read an attribute after commit()?","options":["The cached value from before the commit is returned","The attribute is None until refresh() is called","The ORM tries to reload the row synchronously and raises MissingGreenlet","The read is converted into an awaitable automatically"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Commit expires loaded instances so the next access reloads. That reload is implicit IO the async bridge cannot perform, which is why async sessions set `expire_on_commit=False` or call `refresh()`."}]}]});
